
export const aiengineeringPrompts = [
  {"id":"ai-1","title":"System Prompt Audit for a GPT-4o Customer Support Bot","prompt":"You are a senior prompt engineer at a Series B fintech startup running a GPT-4o-powered customer support bot handling 12,000 daily conversations about loan applications, account disputes, and payment failures. Your system prompt is 1,800 tokens long and you are seeing instruction-following failures on edge cases — the model ignores the 'never speculate about approval odds' constraint about 6% of the time. Conduct a structured audit of the system prompt pasted below. Identify: (1) conflicting instructions that may cause the model to deprioritize constraints, (2) under-specified rules that leave ambiguous wiggle room, (3) sections that exceed 40 tokens without a concrete example and are likely causing instruction fatigue, and (4) missing negative examples that would anchor the model on the identified failure mode. Deliver a rewritten system prompt with inline annotations explaining each change, plus a testing checklist of 10 adversarial user inputs that should now be handled correctly.\n\n[Paste your current prompt or system message]","tags":["prompt-engineering","system-prompt","gpt-4o","fintech","audit"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},

  {"id":"ai-2","title":"Chain-of-Thought Scaffold for Medical Triage Classification","prompt":"You are a prompt engineer contracted by a digital health company building a pre-triage assistant on Claude 3.5 Sonnet. The assistant classifies patient-reported symptoms into four urgency tiers — Emergency, Urgent, Semi-Urgent, Non-Urgent — and the current zero-shot accuracy on your 400-case benchmark is 71%. You need to reach 88% before clinical review. Design a chain-of-thought prompt scaffold that instructs the model to: (1) list all reported symptoms verbatim, (2) check each symptom against a known red-flag list, (3) reason about symptom combinations and duration, (4) output a confidence score between 0 and 1 alongside the classification, and (5) cite the specific reasoning step that drove the final tier assignment. Provide the complete prompt template, three worked few-shot examples covering common misclassification patterns — chest pain without red flags, pediatric fever, and ambiguous abdominal pain — and an evaluation rubric with exact scoring criteria for accuracy, safety recall, and over-triage rate.","tags":["prompt-engineering","chain-of-thought","few-shot","claude","healthcare"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"ai-3","title":"Structured JSON Output Enforcement for LangChain Contract Extraction","prompt":"You are a backend AI engineer at a legal tech company using LangChain with GPT-4o to extract structured contract metadata — party names, governing law, payment terms, termination clauses, and effective dates — from PDFs averaging 45 pages. Your current extraction pipeline has a 14% JSON schema violation rate that breaks downstream ingestion into your PostgreSQL contracts database. The violations fall into three patterns: missing optional fields returned as empty strings instead of null, nested arrays collapsed into comma-separated strings, and dates inconsistently rendered as ISO 8601 vs. US locale. Write a system prompt that enforces strict JSON schema compliance using: (1) explicit field-type declarations with examples for each field, (2) a validation checklist the model must complete before outputting JSON, (3) a fallback instruction for genuinely missing data, and (4) a self-check step where the model re-reads its output against the schema before finalizing. Include the complete JSON schema definition, the full system prompt text, and a LangChain Python snippet wiring in Pydantic validation as a second enforcement layer.","tags":["prompt-engineering","structured-output","json","langchain","legal-tech"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:10:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"ai-4","title":"Few-Shot Prompt Design for B2B Sales Email Tone Calibration","prompt":"You are a prompt engineer at a marketing automation SaaS company building a Claude 3.5 Sonnet-powered email generation feature for B2B sales reps. A/B testing shows the model defaults to overly formal language achieving only a 2.1% reply rate versus the human-written control at 4.8%. Sales leadership identified that top-performing human emails share three characteristics: they open with a specific operational pain point rather than a compliment, they keep sentences under 18 words on average, and they end with a low-commitment call to action. Design a few-shot prompt with exactly five demonstration examples teaching these three characteristics. Each example must span a different vertical — SaaS, logistics, manufacturing, healthcare, financial services — to prevent vertical-specific overfitting. After the examples, write an instruction block that explicitly names the three rules and includes a self-evaluation step where the model scores its own draft on each rule before sending output. Provide the complete prompt and an explanation of why each example was chosen.","tags":["prompt-engineering","few-shot","sales","tone-calibration","claude"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:15:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"ai-5","title":"Meta-Prompt Generator for Iterative Prompt Optimization","prompt":"You are a prompt engineer at an AI product agency shipping ten LLM-powered features per quarter using GPT-4o, Claude 3.5 Sonnet, and Gemini 1.5 Pro. You are building an internal meta-prompt system — a prompt that takes a poorly performing production prompt as input and outputs three improved variants with explanations. The system must handle prompts from four domains: customer support, document summarization, code review, and data extraction. For each improved variant the output must include: (1) the rewritten prompt in full, (2) a change log listing every modification and the hypothesized reason it improves performance, (3) a risk assessment of what failure modes the change might introduce, and (4) five test cases that would distinguish the variant's behavior from the original. Write the complete meta-prompt system instruction, then demonstrate it by running it against the underperforming prompt: 'Summarize this document in 3 bullet points.' Show all three variants with full annotations.\n\n[Paste your current prompt or system message]","tags":["prompt-engineering","meta-prompt","optimization","multi-model","gpt-4o"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:20:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"ai-6","title":"Token-Efficient Prompt Compression for High-Volume API Usage","prompt":"You are a platform engineer at a consumer AI app company running 4 million GPT-4o-mini completions per day with an average system prompt length of 620 tokens. Reducing average system prompt length by 200 tokens would save approximately $14,000 per month. Apply five compression techniques systematically to a set of ten system prompts: (1) replace multi-sentence explanations with imperative directives, (2) convert example-heavy sections into a single canonical example with a note covering variants, (3) remove redundant negative constraints already implied by positive constraints, (4) collapse numbered lists into structured shorthand where order is not meaningful, and (5) eliminate preamble sentences that restate the model's role without adding behavioral instruction. For each technique, show before and after token counts using tiktoken cl100k_base encoding, calculate the compression ratio, and flag any instruction that cannot be compressed without meaningful behavior change. Deliver a compression report and the final optimized prompts.","tags":["prompt-engineering","token-optimization","cost-reduction","gpt-4o-mini","efficiency"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-7","title":"LLM Evaluation Framework from Scratch","prompt":"Build a comprehensive evaluation framework for a question-answering LLM deployed in a healthcare information context. The model must be accurate, safe, and appropriately cautious about medical advice. Design: (1) Automated metrics — implement RAGAS faithfulness and answer relevance scores, BERTScore for semantic accuracy, and a custom refusal-rate metric; (2) LLM-as-judge setup — write the judge prompt with a 5-point rubric for medical appropriateness, include calibration examples for each score level; (3) Human evaluation protocol — annotator guidelines, inter-annotator agreement calculation (Cohen's kappa), sample size determination for statistical significance; (4) Red-team test suite — 50 adversarial question categories including symptom fishing, dosage requests, and diagnosis seeking; (5) Regression testing — define the baseline comparison method and the threshold for blocking a new model release. Provide Python pseudocode for the evaluation pipeline orchestration.","tags":["evals","qa","healthcare"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-8","title":"Embedding Model Selection and Fine-Tuning Strategy","prompt":"You are selecting and optionally fine-tuning an embedding model for a technical documentation search system covering 800,000 code snippets and API reference pages in Python, JavaScript, and Go. Users search with natural language questions like 'how do I handle pagination in the REST client'. Compare these embedding approaches: OpenAI text-embedding-3-large, Cohere embed-v3, E5-large-v2 (self-hosted), and a domain-fine-tuned version of BGE-M3. Evaluation criteria: MTEB benchmark relevance to code retrieval tasks, cost per embedding at 800K docs, latency for batch embedding jobs, multilingual support needs, and self-hosting feasibility. Design a fine-tuning strategy using contrastive learning (SimCSE or MNRL) with hard negatives mined from user click data. Include: triplet dataset construction from search logs, training configuration (batch size, learning rate, epochs), evaluation on a held-out set using Recall@10, and A/B test design to measure retrieval improvement in production.","tags":["embeddings","rag","fine-tuning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-9","title":"Chain-of-Thought Prompt Architecture","prompt":"You are a prompt engineer designing a complex reasoning system for a logistics optimization assistant that must solve vehicle routing problems, explain its reasoning to non-technical dispatchers, and flag edge cases for human review. Design a chain-of-thought prompt architecture that: (1) structures the reasoning into explicit phases (problem decomposition, constraint identification, solution generation, confidence assessment); (2) uses XML tags or markdown headers to separate reasoning traces from final outputs; (3) instructs the model to self-verify its solution against each stated constraint before outputting; (4) specifies a confidence scoring mechanism (1-5) with criteria for each level; (5) defines the escalation trigger when confidence falls below 3. Provide the complete system prompt, an example user message with a 5-stop routing problem, and the ideal model response demonstrating the reasoning structure. Explain why each architectural choice improves reliability over a simple instruction-following prompt.","tags":["prompt-engineering","reasoning","chain-of-thought"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-10","title":"AI Safety Red-Teaming Methodology","prompt":"You are leading an AI safety red-team exercise for a customer-facing LLM deployed at a fintech company. The model handles account inquiries, transaction disputes, and financial guidance. Design a comprehensive red-team methodology covering: (1) Threat model — categorize attack surfaces (prompt injection via user input, indirect injection via retrieved documents, jailbreak via roleplay, data extraction attacks); (2) Attack taxonomy — define 8 attack categories with 5 test cases each (totaling 40 adversarial prompts), including multi-turn escalation attacks; (3) Severity scoring — CVSS-inspired rubric for rating vulnerabilities (likelihood × impact); (4) Testing protocol — how to run attacks safely without exposing real user data; (5) Remediation patterns — for each vulnerability category, specify the defensive system prompt patch; (6) Continuous monitoring — define production signals that indicate an attack is occurring (unusual refusal patterns, specific keyword triggers). Provide 10 example adversarial prompts with expected failure modes and remediation.","tags":["ai-safety","security","red-teaming"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:45:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"aie-11","title":"LangChain Production Architecture Audit","prompt":"[Describe your AI use case and constraints]\n\nYou are a senior LangChain architect. Given the use case and constraints described above, design a production-ready LangChain implementation. Address: (1) Chain vs. LCEL (LangChain Expression Language) — recommend the appropriate abstraction and justify based on the use case complexity and streaming requirements; (2) Memory management — compare ConversationBufferMemory, ConversationSummaryMemory, and VectorStoreRetrieverMemory for this context; (3) Retriever configuration — if RAG is needed, specify the retriever type, search parameters, and reranking strategy; (4) Output parsing — design a Pydantic output schema that matches the use case; (5) Error handling — define retry logic, fallback chains, and graceful degradation; (6) Observability — LangSmith tracing setup with custom tags; (7) Testing — unit test patterns for chain components. Provide complete Python code skeletons for the core chain and retriever setup, with comments explaining production considerations.","tags":["langchain","architecture","production"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-12","title":"Multimodal AI Pipeline Design","prompt":"Design a production multimodal AI pipeline for an e-commerce platform that needs to: accept product images and text descriptions from sellers, generate standardized product listings with extracted attributes (color, size, material, category), detect quality issues in images (blurry, watermarked, inappropriate content), and route complex cases to human reviewers. Architecture requirements: process 100,000 product submissions per day, P95 latency under 10 seconds, support for image+text and image-only inputs, structured JSON output for catalog ingestion. Specify: vision model selection (GPT-4o vs. Claude 3.5 Sonnet vs. LLaVA self-hosted) with cost-accuracy trade-off analysis, image preprocessing pipeline (resizing, format normalization, quality scoring), multimodal prompt design with few-shot examples embedded, output schema with confidence scores per attribute, human review routing logic based on confidence thresholds, and monitoring for model drift on image quality distribution shifts. Include a cost breakdown per 100K submissions.","tags":["multimodal","vision","architecture"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-13","title":"Responsible AI Governance Framework","prompt":"You are the Head of AI Ethics at a company deploying LLMs in three high-stakes domains: hiring screening, loan underwriting, and medical triage support. Design a comprehensive responsible AI governance framework that covers: (1) Bias audit methodology — statistical fairness tests (demographic parity, equalized odds) for each domain, with specific protected attributes and acceptable disparity thresholds; (2) Explainability requirements — when and how to surface model reasoning to affected individuals; (3) Human oversight policy — decision categories that always require human sign-off; (4) Incident response — classification of AI-related incidents by severity and response SLA; (5) Model cards — required documentation fields for each deployed model; (6) Stakeholder communication — how to disclose AI involvement to job applicants, loan applicants, and patients; (7) Regulatory mapping — GDPR Article 22 (automated decision-making), EU AI Act risk categories, and US EEOC guidance applicability. Provide a governance checklist that must be signed off before any new AI feature launches.","tags":["ai-ethics","governance","responsible-ai"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:00:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"aie-14","title":"Prompt Injection Defense Hardening","prompt":"You are a security engineer hardening an LLM-powered document Q&A system against prompt injection attacks. The system retrieves user-uploaded documents and generates answers based on retrieved chunks. An attacker could embed malicious instructions inside uploaded PDFs to manipulate the AI's behavior. Design a multi-layered defense: (1) Input sanitization — rules for stripping or escaping instruction-like text patterns in documents before embedding; (2) Prompt architecture — structural defenses (XML delimiters, canary tokens, privilege separation between system and retrieved content); (3) Output validation — a secondary LLM judge that checks whether the response follows the original system intent; (4) Behavioral anomaly detection — production signals that indicate injection is occurring (sudden persona changes, unexpected tool calls, system prompt echoing); (5) Test suite — 20 injection attack patterns with expected detection outcomes; (6) Incident response — steps to take when an injection attack is detected in production. Include a before/after comparison of the prompt architecture showing how each defense applies.","tags":["security","prompt-engineering","ai-safety"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-15","title":"Token Efficiency Optimization","prompt":"[Paste your current prompt or system instruction]\n\nYou are a prompt optimization specialist. Analyze the system instruction above for token efficiency without degrading task performance. Apply these optimization passes: (1) Semantic compression — identify verbose phrases that can be expressed in fewer tokens with identical meaning; (2) Redundancy elimination — find repeated concepts or overlapping constraints and consolidate them; (3) Structural efficiency — evaluate whether bullet lists, numbered steps, or prose is most token-efficient for each section; (4) Negative constraint pruning — identify negative constraints (things NOT to do) that are either self-evident or contradicted by positive constraints; (5) Example economy — if few-shot examples are present, determine the minimum number needed and remove the least informative ones. For each change, show: original text, revised text, tokens saved (estimate), and risk assessment (could this change degrade performance?). Produce a final optimized version with a total token reduction summary. Flag any optimization that carries medium or high performance risk.","tags":["prompt-engineering","optimization","token-efficiency"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-16","title":"RAG Hallucination Mitigation Strategy","prompt":"Your RAG system is producing answers that blend retrieved facts with fabricated details. Users cannot distinguish which parts of the answer are grounded in source documents. Design a comprehensive hallucination mitigation strategy: (1) Retrieval quality improvements — minimum similarity score thresholds, diversity-enforced retrieval (MMR), and query expansion to improve recall; (2) Prompt-level grounding — system prompt instructions that enforce citation, prohibit extrapolation, and require explicit 'not found in sources' statements; (3) Answer attribution — inline citation format design with chunk ID references, and a post-processing step that highlights uncited claims; (4) Faithfulness scoring — implement an automated faithfulness metric using an LLM judge that compares each claim in the answer against retrieved chunks; (5) Uncertainty communication — design the UX for surfacing low-confidence answers to users; (6) Evaluation dataset — construct a 200-question test set with known-grounded and known-ungrounded question types. Provide example prompt templates and the faithfulness judge prompt with scoring rubric.","tags":["rag","hallucination","reliability"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-17","title":"AI Performance Benchmarking Plan","prompt":"Design a rigorous performance benchmarking plan to compare three LLM candidates (GPT-4o, Claude 3.5 Sonnet, Gemini 1.5 Pro) for deployment in an enterprise legal document review workflow. The workflow involves: contract clause extraction, risk flag identification, summarization of 50-page agreements, and multilingual support for contracts in English, French, German, and Spanish. Benchmarking plan must cover: (1) Task-specific test sets — minimum 200 examples per task with human-annotated ground truth; (2) Metrics per task — extraction F1, risk classification accuracy, summary quality (ROUGE + human rating), translation accuracy (BLEU + human); (3) Latency benchmarking — P50/P90/P99 at 1, 10, and 100 concurrent requests; (4) Cost modeling — total cost per 1,000 documents processed; (5) Consistency testing — same prompt run 5 times to measure output variance; (6) Regression suite — 50 known failure cases from production; (7) Statistical significance — sample size calculation and confidence interval reporting. Provide the evaluation scorecard template and decision criteria for model selection.","tags":["benchmarking","evals","llm"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-18","title":"LlamaIndex Knowledge Graph RAG","prompt":"Design a LlamaIndex-based knowledge graph RAG system for a pharmaceutical company's internal research database containing 500,000 research papers, clinical trial results, and drug interaction records. Standard vector search is insufficient because complex queries require multi-hop reasoning (e.g., 'Find all drugs that interact with Compound X and have been tested in patients with Condition Y'). Architecture specification: (1) Knowledge graph construction — entity extraction pipeline (drug names, compounds, conditions, trial IDs), relationship type taxonomy, and graph storage (Neo4j vs. Nebula Graph); (2) LlamaIndex integration — KnowledgeGraphIndex configuration, custom entity extractor using a fine-tuned NER model, and graph query synthesis; (3) Hybrid retrieval — combining graph traversal results with vector similarity for context assembly; (4) Query routing — classifier that decides between graph query, vector search, or hybrid based on question type; (5) Evaluation — metrics for multi-hop reasoning accuracy; (6) Complete LlamaIndex Python code for the core pipeline. Include an example query trace showing graph traversal steps.","tags":["llamaindex","knowledge-graph","rag"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-19","title":"Model Distillation Strategy","prompt":"You are reducing the inference cost for a large proprietary LLM (GPT-4-class) that handles 10 million queries per month for a B2C app. 80% of queries are simple FAQ-style, 15% are moderate complexity, and 5% are complex reasoning tasks. Design a model distillation and routing strategy: (1) Student model selection — compare fine-tuned Mistral-7B, Phi-3-medium, and Llama-3-8B as distillation targets; (2) Knowledge distillation approach — response distillation (train on teacher outputs) vs. feature distillation; (3) Training data collection — how to use production teacher outputs as training signal with quality filtering; (4) Complexity classifier — design a lightweight router (< 10ms latency) that classifies query complexity and routes to the appropriate model tier; (5) Quality gate — automated evaluation pipeline that validates the student model meets minimum accuracy thresholds before promotion; (6) Expected cost reduction — calculate savings assuming GPT-4o at $15/1M tokens vs. self-hosted 7B model at $0.50/1M tokens at given query volumes. Include the router prompt and classification schema.","tags":["model-distillation","mlops","cost-optimization"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-20","title":"Structured Output Enforcement System","prompt":"You are designing a robust structured output system for an LLM that must return complex nested JSON for downstream API consumption. The output schema includes: a risk assessment object with nested arrays, confidence scores for each field, optional sub-objects that appear conditionally, and an enum field with 12 possible values. The system must handle cases where the model produces malformed JSON, truncated responses, or valid JSON that violates the schema. Design: (1) Prompt-level enforcement — system prompt design that maximizes schema compliance, including schema injection strategy and field-by-field instruction; (2) Grammar-based constrained decoding — configure llama.cpp or vLLM's JSON mode / function calling to constrain the token generation; (3) Validation and repair pipeline — Pydantic schema validation, automated repair prompting for schema violations, and fallback strategy for unrecoverable outputs; (4) Testing — schema compliance rate benchmark across 1,000 test cases; (5) Complete Python code for the validation/repair pipeline. Include the Pydantic model definition and the repair prompt template.","tags":["structured-output","json","prompt-engineering"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-21","title":"Agentic Loop Design with Tool Use","prompt":"Design the agentic loop architecture for an AI research assistant that autonomously answers complex business intelligence questions by using tools: web search, SQL database query, Python code execution, and a document retrieval API. The agent must handle queries like 'What is our market share trend versus Competitor X over the last 3 years, and what are the top 3 analyst explanations?' Specify: (1) ReAct (Reason + Act) loop structure with explicit thought/action/observation formatting; (2) Tool definitions — complete JSON schema for each of the 4 tools with parameter descriptions and example calls; (3) Planning step — initial query decomposition before tool use begins; (4) State management — how intermediate results accumulate across tool calls; (5) Loop termination — conditions for concluding vs. continuing tool use; (6) Error recovery — what happens when a tool call fails or returns unexpected data; (7) Safety guardrails — limits on loop iterations, tool call rate limiting, and dangerous action prevention; (8) Complete system prompt for the agent. Provide a full worked example trace for the sample query above.","tags":["agents","tool-use","react"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:40:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"aie-22","title":"Semantic Chunking Strategy for RAG","prompt":"You are optimizing the document processing pipeline for a RAG system serving a financial services knowledge base. The corpus includes: 10-K annual reports (100+ pages), short news articles (300-500 words), regulatory circulars (dense legal text), and Excel-derived data tables embedded in PDFs. Fixed-size chunking is producing poor retrieval quality because it splits sentences and tables mid-way. Design an advanced chunking strategy: (1) Semantic chunking — algorithm for splitting on semantic boundaries (sentences, paragraphs, sections) rather than fixed token counts; (2) Document-type routing — different chunking strategies for each document type (reports vs. articles vs. tables); (3) Table extraction — specialized handling for financial tables preserving row/column relationships; (4) Chunk metadata schema — what metadata to attach to each chunk (document ID, section heading path, page number, date, entity tags); (5) Chunk size optimization — empirical method for finding the optimal chunk size using retrieval recall on a held-out query set; (6) Parent-child chunking — store small chunks for retrieval but return larger parent context to the LLM. Provide Python pseudocode for the semantic chunking algorithm.","tags":["rag","chunking","document-processing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-23","title":"LLM Latency Optimization Playbook","prompt":"Your LLM API serving layer has a P95 latency of 8 seconds for a customer-facing chat feature. The target is P95 under 2 seconds. The current setup: GPT-4o with 2,000-token system prompt, average 500-token user message, average 800-token response, served via a Python FastAPI backend with no caching. Diagnose and resolve the latency problem with a full optimization playbook: (1) Prompt caching — implement OpenAI's prompt caching feature and calculate expected TTFT reduction; (2) Response streaming — implement server-sent events for token-by-token streaming and the frontend UX changes needed; (3) Model tier routing — identify which query types can be served by a faster, cheaper model; (4) System prompt compression — reduce from 2,000 to 600 tokens without capability loss using the techniques described; (5) Parallel tool calls — restructure any sequential tool calls into parallel execution; (6) Infrastructure — async Python client configuration, connection pooling, and retry-with-backoff settings; (7) Speculative decoding — explain the technique and when to apply it. Provide the before/after latency estimate for each optimization with implementation priority order.","tags":["latency","optimization","mlops"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-24","title":"Fine-Tuning vs. RAG Decision Framework","prompt":"You are advising a product team deciding between fine-tuning and RAG for injecting domain knowledge into an LLM product. The use case: an AI assistant for insurance adjusters that needs deep knowledge of 3,000 product manuals, policy documents, and claims procedures, combined with conversational ability to guide adjusters through complex claim assessments. Build a decision framework: (1) Knowledge type analysis — categorize the 3,000 documents into: frequently updated content, stable procedural knowledge, and factual reference material, explaining which type favors RAG vs. fine-tuning; (2) RAG strengths and limits for this use case — what types of queries RAG handles well vs. where it fails; (3) Fine-tuning strengths and limits — behavior and style changes fine-tuning can achieve vs. knowledge injection limitations; (4) Hybrid architecture — design a system that uses fine-tuning for tone/behavior and RAG for factual retrieval; (5) Cost comparison — one-time fine-tuning cost vs. ongoing RAG infrastructure; (6) Maintenance burden — update frequency requirements for each approach. Provide a scored decision matrix and a final recommendation with implementation roadmap.","tags":["fine-tuning","rag","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-25","title":"AI Product Metrics and KPI Framework","prompt":"You are the Head of AI Product at a B2B SaaS company that has just launched an AI writing assistant for marketing teams. Define a comprehensive metrics framework to measure product success, model quality, and business impact. Structure the framework across four layers: (1) Model quality metrics — task completion rate, factual accuracy score, instruction following rate, output consistency (variance across repeated prompts), and refusal rate (both appropriate and inappropriate refusals); (2) User experience metrics — time-to-first-useful-output, edit distance between AI output and final published content, user acceptance rate (published without major edit), and user satisfaction (CSAT, NPS); (3) Business metrics — weekly active AI feature users, AI-assisted content volume, time saved per content piece, and premium tier conversion attributed to AI features; (4) Safety and compliance metrics — policy violation rate, sensitive content detection rate, and audit trail completeness. For each metric: define the measurement method, data source, target value at 90 days post-launch, and the alert threshold that triggers investigation. Include a dashboard layout specification.","tags":["ai-product","metrics","kpi"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-26","title":"Hybrid Search Implementation","prompt":"Implement a hybrid search system combining dense vector search and sparse BM25 keyword search for a technical support knowledge base. The system must handle both conceptual queries ('how do I improve model performance') and exact-match queries ('error code E-4021'). Design: (1) Architecture — parallel query execution against both a vector index (Qdrant) and an Elasticsearch BM25 index, with a fusion layer; (2) Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) — implement the RRF score combination formula and justify the k parameter choice; (3) Query type classification — lightweight classifier to adjust dense/sparse weighting based on query characteristics (keyword-heavy vs. semantic); (4) Re-ranking — add a cross-encoder re-ranker (ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2) as a final pass on the top-20 fused results; (5) Index synchronization — keeping both indexes in sync when documents are added or updated; (6) Evaluation — design an A/B test comparing hybrid vs. vector-only search using NDCG@10 and user click-through rate; (7) Complete Python implementation code for the hybrid search function including RRF fusion and re-ranking steps.","tags":["rag","hybrid-search","information-retrieval"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-27","title":"LLM Output Monitoring and Drift Detection","prompt":"Design a production monitoring system for LLM outputs that detects quality degradation, behavioral drift, and safety violations without human review of every response. The system processes 500,000 responses per day across 12 different AI features. Architecture: (1) Sampling strategy — risk-based sampling (higher rate for sensitive topics, new users, edge-case query types) to keep review volume manageable; (2) Automated quality signals — perplexity scoring, response length distribution, format compliance rate, and semantic similarity to golden examples; (3) Safety classifier — lightweight fine-tuned classifier for detecting policy violations in outputs (faster and cheaper than using a frontier LLM as judge); (4) Statistical drift detection — CUSUM or Page-Hinkley test applied to quality metrics time series; (5) Alert taxonomy — severity levels (P1: immediate action, P2: same-day review, P3: weekly review); (6) Root cause tooling — when drift is detected, how to trace back to prompt version changes, model API changes, or input distribution shifts; (7) Dashboard specification. Provide the drift detection algorithm pseudocode and alert configuration.","tags":["monitoring","mlops","drift-detection"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-28","title":"Reasoning Model Prompting Techniques","prompt":"You are optimizing prompts for a reasoning-class model (OpenAI o1/o3 or DeepSeek-R1 style) that performs extended chain-of-thought reasoning before answering. These models differ fundamentally from standard instruction-following models in how they respond to prompting. Design a prompting guide for reasoning models covering: (1) Prompt brevity — why shorter, clearer prompts outperform elaborate instruction sets on reasoning models; (2) Avoiding CoT elicitation — why you should not ask these models to 'think step by step' (they already do); (3) Constraint specification — how to frame constraints so the model's internal reasoning respects them; (4) Output format control — techniques for getting structured output when the model wants to reason freely; (5) Use case fit — task types where reasoning models excel vs. where standard models are better and cheaper; (6) Budget tokens — strategies for controlling reasoning depth vs. output length; (7) Side-by-side examples — show the same 5 prompts written for a standard model vs. rewritten for a reasoning model with performance notes. Include a decision tree for choosing between reasoning and standard models.","tags":["reasoning-models","prompt-engineering","llm"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-29","title":"AI System Design Interview: Recommendation Engine","prompt":"You are conducting a system design interview. The candidate must design an AI-powered content recommendation engine for a news platform with 5 million daily active users. Walk through the complete system design as an interviewer would expect: (1) Requirements clarification — functional requirements (personalization, diversity, freshness), non-functional requirements (latency < 200ms, availability 99.99%); (2) Data model — user interaction schema, article feature schema, embedding storage; (3) ML model architecture — two-tower neural network for candidate generation, lightweight ranking model for final ordering; (4) Feature engineering — user features (read history embeddings, topic preferences), item features (article embeddings, recency decay, popularity score); (5) Training pipeline — data collection, negative sampling strategy, online vs. offline training trade-offs; (6) Serving infrastructure — candidate retrieval via ANN search, feature serving with low-latency cache, A/B testing framework; (7) Cold start problem — strategy for new users and new articles; (8) Evaluation — offline metrics (NDCG, diversity) and online metrics (CTR, session length). Provide a system architecture diagram description and discuss trade-offs at each major decision point.","tags":["system-design","recommendation","ml"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-30","title":"Conversational AI Memory Architecture","prompt":"Design the memory architecture for a long-running conversational AI assistant used by enterprise sales teams. Sales reps have ongoing relationships with the assistant spanning hundreds of conversations over months, and the assistant must remember: account-specific details (company names, deal stages, key contacts), user preferences (communication style, report formats), past analytical conclusions, and instructions the user has given ('always show competitor data when I ask about win rate'). Design the complete memory system: (1) Memory taxonomy — working memory (current conversation), episodic memory (past conversation summaries), semantic memory (extracted facts and preferences), procedural memory (user-defined instructions); (2) Storage architecture — what goes in the context window vs. a vector store vs. a relational database; (3) Memory consolidation — how to compress episodic memory over time without losing salient details; (4) Retrieval strategy — how to decide which memories are relevant to the current conversation turn; (5) Memory update pipeline — how new information is extracted, validated, and stored; (6) Forgetting policy — GDPR-compliant memory deletion and TTL policies. Provide the complete data schemas and a Python class skeleton for the memory manager.","tags":["memory","agents","conversational-ai"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-31","title":"RLHF and Preference Learning Pipeline","prompt":"Design a complete RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback) pipeline for improving a code generation model's alignment with developer preferences. The base model generates syntactically correct code but produces solutions that experienced developers rate as 'unidiomatic', 'overly complex', or 'missing error handling'. Pipeline design: (1) Preference data collection — annotation interface design, annotator qualification criteria, prompt selection strategy (diverse tasks, difficulty levels), pairwise comparison vs. absolute rating trade-offs; (2) Reward model training — architecture choice (same base model with regression head vs. separate smaller model), dataset size requirements, reward model evaluation; (3) PPO training configuration — KL divergence penalty tuning, reward clipping, value function setup, batch size and epoch recommendations; (4) DPO alternative — explain Direct Preference Optimization as a simpler alternative to PPO and when to prefer it; (5) Evaluation — before/after comparison using human win rate, coding benchmark scores (HumanEval, MBPP), and safety regression testing; (6) Iteration cadence — how often to collect new preferences and retrain. Include training configuration examples for both PPO and DPO approaches.","tags":["rlhf","fine-tuning","alignment"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-32","title":"AI Cost Optimization at Scale","prompt":"Your company is spending $180,000/month on LLM API costs across 15 AI features. The CTO has asked for a plan to reduce costs by 40% without degrading user-facing quality. Conduct a systematic cost optimization analysis: (1) Cost attribution — framework for breaking down cost by feature, query type, and model tier to identify the highest-ROI optimization targets; (2) Prompt caching — calculate savings from caching static system prompt prefixes; (3) Model tiering — classify each of the 15 features by required intelligence level and map to the cheapest model that meets the quality bar; (4) Output length control — techniques for reducing average response length (max_tokens limits, summarization instructions, structured outputs vs. prose); (5) Batch processing — identify features that don't require real-time response and can be batched at off-peak hours for 50% API discounts; (6) Self-hosted inference — ROI calculation for deploying open-source models for the highest-volume, lower-complexity features; (7) Caching layer — semantic caching for frequently repeated queries using embedding similarity. Produce a prioritized savings roadmap with implementation effort vs. savings for each initiative.","tags":["cost-optimization","mlops","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-33","title":"Synthetic Data Generation for Edge Cases","prompt":"Your AI classifier for detecting financial fraud in transaction descriptions has poor recall on rare fraud patterns because the training data contains fewer than 50 examples of each rare category. Design a synthetic data generation pipeline to augment the training set: (1) Generation strategy — use GPT-4o with detailed fraud pattern descriptions to generate synthetic examples; (2) Prompt design — write the generation prompt that produces diverse, realistic transaction descriptions while staying within the statistical properties of each fraud category; (3) Quality filtering — semantic similarity filter to remove near-duplicates, human review sampling rate, and automated quality score using a separate classifier; (4) Distribution balancing — how many synthetic examples to add per category to achieve target class ratios without over-representation; (5) Contamination prevention — techniques to ensure synthetic data doesn't leak into validation or test sets; (6) Evaluation — measure the impact of synthetic augmentation on recall for rare classes using stratified k-fold cross-validation; (7) Ongoing pipeline — automated synthetic data refresh when new fraud patterns are identified. Include the generation prompt template and quality filtering pseudocode.","tags":["synthetic-data","fine-tuning","data-engineering"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-34","title":"Multi-Tenant AI System Architecture","prompt":"Design a multi-tenant LLM platform that serves 200 enterprise customers with isolated configurations, data, and model behaviors. Each customer requires: custom system prompts and personas, private knowledge bases (RAG), separate fine-tuned model variants for some customers, data isolation guarantees, and per-customer usage billing. Architecture: (1) Tenant isolation model — logical isolation (shared infrastructure, tenant-prefixed namespaces) vs. physical isolation (dedicated vector stores and model endpoints) with trade-off analysis; (2) Configuration management — tenant config schema including system prompts, RAG settings, model selection, feature flags, and safety policy overrides; (3) Vector store isolation — approach for keeping each tenant's document embeddings separate and preventing cross-tenant retrieval; (4) Model serving — how to efficiently serve custom fine-tuned adapters (LoRA) per tenant without spinning up separate model instances; (5) Auth and access control — API key scoping, tenant context injection, and audit logging; (6) Cost isolation — usage metering architecture for per-tenant billing; (7) Onboarding pipeline — automated provisioning workflow for new tenants. Provide the tenant config schema and the request routing pseudocode.","tags":["multi-tenant","architecture","saas"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-35","title":"Prompt Regression Testing System","prompt":"Design a prompt regression testing system for an engineering team that manages 80 production prompts across a customer service platform. When a prompt is updated or when the underlying LLM API changes versions, the team needs automated confidence that quality hasn't degraded. System design: (1) Test case library — schema for storing test cases (input, expected output characteristics, evaluation criteria type); (2) Evaluation criteria types — exact match, semantic similarity threshold, rubric-based LLM judge, regex pattern match, JSON schema validation; (3) CI/CD integration — GitHub Actions workflow that runs the test suite on every prompt PR and blocks merge on regression; (4) Baseline management — how to update baselines when intentional improvements are made vs. detecting unintended regressions; (5) LLM version change testing — automated pipeline that runs the full suite when the API provider announces a model update; (6) Flakiness handling — strategy for non-deterministic outputs (temperature > 0), including multi-sample aggregation; (7) Reporting — dashboard showing pass/fail trends per prompt and aggregate quality score over time. Include the test case schema, evaluation function signatures, and CI/CD workflow YAML.","tags":["testing","prompt-engineering","mlops"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-36","title":"LoRA Fine-Tuning Configuration Guide","prompt":"You are fine-tuning Llama-3-8B-Instruct with LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) for a specialized tax advisory assistant. The dataset is 15,000 high-quality Q&A pairs in tax law. Provide a complete LoRA fine-tuning configuration guide: (1) LoRA hyperparameters — rank (r), alpha, dropout, and which transformer modules to target (q_proj, v_proj, k_proj, o_proj, gate_proj — justify each choice); (2) Training hyperparameters — learning rate schedule (cosine with warmup), batch size, gradient accumulation, number of epochs, and early stopping criteria; (3) Quantization — QLoRA 4-bit quantization setup using bitsandbytes, expected memory footprint on A100 80GB; (4) Data formatting — chat template formatting for Llama-3-Instruct, special token handling; (5) Evaluation during training — perplexity on validation set, task-specific accuracy metrics evaluated every N steps; (6) Catastrophic forgetting — measurement approach and mitigation (replay buffer, regularization); (7) Merging and export — merge LoRA weights into base model, quantize to GGUF for deployment, vLLM serving configuration. Provide the complete training script configuration using the Hugging Face TRL library (SFTTrainer).","tags":["fine-tuning","lora","llm"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:55:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"aie-37","title":"AI Feature Flag and Rollout Strategy","prompt":"Design the feature flag and gradual rollout strategy for a new AI feature (AI-generated email drafts) being added to a 2 million user email client. The AI feature uses a new LLM integration that must be validated at scale before full rollout, and different user segments may have different quality experiences. Rollout design: (1) Flag architecture — feature flag schema supporting percentage rollout, user attribute targeting (subscription tier, language, usage frequency), and kill switch; (2) Rollout stages — 0.1% internal users → 1% beta users → 5% → 25% → 100%, with specific success/failure criteria at each gate; (3) Metrics for go/no-go — acceptance rate, quality rating, error rate, latency P95, and cost per user per day; (4) Holdout group — maintain a permanent holdout for long-term impact measurement; (5) Experimentation — A/B test framework to compare AI draft variants (different prompts, different models); (6) Observability — per-segment quality dashboards and automated anomaly alerts; (7) Rollback procedure — automatic rollback triggers and manual override process. Provide the feature flag schema and the rollout decision criteria table.","tags":["mlops","feature-flags","rollout"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-38","title":"Agentic AI Safety Guardrails","prompt":"Design a comprehensive safety guardrail system for an autonomous AI agent that has access to: web browsing, code execution (Python sandbox), email sending, calendar management, and file system access. The agent operates with minimal human supervision for extended tasks. Safety architecture: (1) Permission model — define a permission tier system (read-only, reversible writes, irreversible actions) with escalation requirements; (2) Action whitelist/blacklist — explicit allowed and forbidden action categories with examples for each tool; (3) Pre-execution validation — before executing any action, the agent must pass through a safety check layer that evaluates: is this action consistent with the original task goal? does this action have irreversible consequences? does this action affect third parties?; (4) Scope creep detection — mechanism for detecting when the agent is pursuing actions outside the originally specified task scope; (5) Rate limits — maximum actions per minute per tool to prevent runaway loops; (6) Audit logging — complete structured log of every action, reasoning, and tool output; (7) Human escalation protocol — specific scenarios that always require human approval before proceeding. Provide the safety check system prompt and the permission schema.","tags":["ai-safety","agents","guardrails"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:05:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"aie-39","title":"Context Window Utilization Strategy","prompt":"You are designing the context window utilization strategy for a long-document analysis agent working with 200-page technical reports. The model has a 128K token context window, but naive approaches (dump the whole document) produce poor results due to lost-in-the-middle effects. Design an optimal context utilization strategy: (1) Relevance-weighted context assembly — retrieve the most relevant chunks via RAG but also include document structure markers (table of contents, section headers) to help the model navigate; (2) Position-aware placement — empirical evidence on where to place critical information (beginning, middle, end of context) based on the lost-in-the-middle research, and how to structure content accordingly; (3) Dynamic context allocation — algorithm for deciding how many tokens to allocate to: retrieved chunks, conversation history, system instructions, and output space; (4) Progressive summarization — for very long documents, a tiered reading approach (skim entire doc → deep read relevant sections); (5) Context freshness — in multi-turn conversations, strategy for aging out old context that's no longer relevant; (6) Evaluation — methodology for measuring whether context utilization improvements actually improve answer quality. Provide the context assembly algorithm pseudocode.","tags":["context-window","rag","prompt-engineering"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-40","title":"AI Ethics in Hiring Tools","prompt":"You are the AI ethics lead reviewing an AI-assisted candidate screening tool before deployment. The tool uses an LLM to score resumes and generate interview question recommendations for 10,000 job applications per month. Conduct a comprehensive ethics review: (1) Bias audit design — protected attribute analysis (age, gender, name-based ethnicity inference), disparate impact testing methodology, and acceptable thresholds; (2) Proxy variable detection — identify resume features that correlate with protected attributes (graduation year → age, university prestige → socioeconomic background) and how to mitigate them; (3) Explainability requirements — what explanation must be provided to rejected candidates under EEOC and EU AI Act requirements; (4) Human oversight — which decisions must remain with human reviewers, and what information the AI can vs. cannot provide; (5) Feedback loop risk — how AI screening scores could create self-reinforcing bias in future training data; (6) Audit trail — record-keeping requirements for demonstrating non-discriminatory use; (7) Vendor assessment — if using a third-party AI tool, what contractual and technical assurances are required. Provide a deployment readiness checklist and three specific prompt modifications that reduce bias.","tags":["ai-ethics","bias","hiring"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-41","title":"Retrieval Quality Debugging Workflow","prompt":"[Describe your AI use case and constraints]\n\nYour RAG system is returning irrelevant or incomplete information for a significant percentage of queries. Given the use case described above, design a systematic debugging workflow to diagnose and fix retrieval quality issues. Debugging process: (1) Diagnostic taxonomy — categorize retrieval failures into: vocabulary mismatch (user query uses different terms than documents), semantic gap (query concept not well-represented in embedding space), chunking artifacts (answer split across chunk boundaries), coverage gaps (information not in the knowledge base), and ranking errors (right document retrieved but ranked too low); (2) Failure analysis pipeline — how to sample and manually analyze 100 failed queries to classify failure types; (3) Query analysis tools — query expansion techniques (HyDE — Hypothetical Document Embeddings, multi-query generation), and when to apply each; (4) Chunk boundary fixes — re-chunking strategy to fix sentence/answer splitting; (5) Embedding debugging — visualize embedding space with UMAP to identify clustering issues; (6) Re-ranking tuning — evaluate whether adding a cross-encoder re-ranker resolves ranking failures; (7) Evaluation before/after — NDCG@5 measurement to confirm improvements. Provide the failure classification rubric and query expansion prompt templates.","tags":["rag","debugging","retrieval"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-42","title":"LLM API Abstraction Layer Design","prompt":"Design an LLM API abstraction layer for an enterprise platform that needs to: switch between OpenAI, Anthropic, Azure OpenAI, and self-hosted Ollama without changing application code, implement fallback chains (try primary provider, fall back to secondary on error), enforce rate limits and cost budgets per team, log all requests for compliance, and add prompt caching transparently. Architecture: (1) Provider adapter pattern — abstract interface definition with concrete adapters for each provider, handling API differences (message format, streaming, function calling schema differences); (2) Fallback chain — configuration-driven fallback with exponential backoff and circuit breaker pattern; (3) Rate limiting — token bucket algorithm implementation for rate limits per API key and per team; (4) Cost tracking — real-time cost accumulation with budget alerts and hard stops; (5) Prompt caching layer — semantic cache using embedding similarity to return cached responses for near-duplicate queries; (6) Observability — structured logging schema capturing: provider, model, latency, tokens, cost, cache hit/miss; (7) Configuration management — how teams configure their model preferences without code changes. Provide the abstract interface definition and the fallback chain implementation in Python.","tags":["architecture","api","mlops"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-43","title":"Quantization and Model Compression Guide","prompt":"You are preparing a 70B parameter LLM for deployment on a 4x A100 80GB server cluster that will serve 500 concurrent users. The full-precision model requires 140GB just for weights and cannot fit. Design a quantization and compression strategy: (1) Quantization methods comparison — FP16 vs. BF16 vs. INT8 (LLM.int8()) vs. GPTQ INT4 vs. AWQ INT4 — accuracy-memory-speed trade-offs for each; (2) Recommended configuration — select the optimal quantization level for this use case with justification, expected memory footprint calculation, and expected quality degradation; (3) Tensor parallelism — how to shard the model across 4 GPUs using tensor parallelism, pipeline parallelism, or a hybrid; (4) vLLM deployment — vLLM configuration for the quantized model including PagedAttention, continuous batching settings, and max batch size; (5) Benchmark plan — how to measure tokens/second throughput and latency at 500 concurrent users using a load testing tool; (6) Quality validation — MMLU and domain-specific benchmark comparison between full-precision and quantized model; (7) Cost calculation — GPU instance costs vs. API costs at target usage volume. Provide the vLLM launch command and quantization configuration.","tags":["quantization","inference","deployment"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-44","title":"Tool Calling Schema Design","prompt":"Design a comprehensive tool calling schema for a financial analysis AI agent. The agent needs to interact with: a market data API, a SQL database of company financials, a Python code interpreter for calculations, a news search API, and an internal document retrieval system. Tool schema design: (1) Function definition best practices — how to write function names, descriptions, and parameter descriptions that maximize the LLM's correct tool selection rate; (2) Parameter design — required vs. optional parameters, enum types for constrained choices, complex nested objects; (3) Tool selection guidance — when to include tool usage hints in the system prompt vs. relying on the model to infer; (4) Parallel tool calls — identify which tools can be called in parallel and how to structure the agent loop to exploit parallelism; (5) Return value handling — how to format tool outputs for optimal LLM comprehension, handling large data returns (pagination, summarization); (6) Error response format — standardized error schema that helps the LLM understand failures and retry correctly; (7) Complete JSON schemas for all 5 tools. Include a worked example showing the agent's tool selection trace for a query like 'Compare revenue growth of AAPL, MSFT, and GOOGL over 5 years with analyst consensus context'.","tags":["tool-calling","agents","function-calling"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-45","title":"Semantic Search vs. Full-Text Search Decision Guide","prompt":"You are advising an engineering team choosing between semantic vector search, traditional full-text search (BM25/Elasticsearch), and a hybrid approach for five different internal search use cases: (1) HR policy Q&A — employees asking natural language questions about policies; (2) Code search — developers searching for functions or patterns across 2M lines of codebase; (3) Customer support ticket search — agents looking up similar past tickets by description; (4) Product SKU search — customers searching by exact product names, model numbers, and specifications; (5) Research paper search — scientists searching across 50,000 internal research documents by concept. For each use case: analyze the query characteristics (keyword-heavy vs. conceptual, exact-match needs, domain vocabulary), recommend the search approach with justification, specify the embedding model or BM25 configuration appropriate for that domain, define the evaluation metric (Recall@K, MRR, NDCG), and estimate infrastructure cost. Conclude with a general decision framework (flowchart description) for choosing search approach given query and document characteristics.","tags":["search","embeddings","architecture"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-46","title":"AI Incident Response Runbook","prompt":"Create a comprehensive AI incident response runbook for a team operating LLM-powered features in a consumer application with 1 million daily users. Cover five incident categories: (1) Safety violation — the model produces harmful, toxic, or policy-violating content at scale; (2) Hallucination surge — accuracy metrics drop below threshold, model is producing false information; (3) Latency spike — P95 latency exceeds SLA, users experiencing timeouts; (4) Data leak — model potentially outputting information from other users' conversations; (5) Cost runaway — API costs spike 10x due to infinite loops, token bloat, or abuse. For each incident type: define the detection signal (what metric or alert triggers this), immediate containment action (kill switch, rate limit, model rollback), investigation steps (what logs to pull, what to analyze), communication template (internal Slack message, user-facing status page), resolution criteria, and post-incident review checklist. Include the on-call rotation setup, escalation matrix, and the monitoring dashboard panels that should be visible during an incident.","tags":["incident-response","mlops","operations"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-47","title":"Knowledge Base Maintenance Pipeline","prompt":"Design an automated pipeline for maintaining a RAG knowledge base that must stay current with 500 new and updated documents per day from multiple sources (internal wiki, Confluence, SharePoint, web crawl). The knowledge base serves a product support chatbot where outdated information causes incorrect answers. Pipeline design: (1) Source connectors — integration patterns for each source type with change detection (webhook vs. polling vs. content hash comparison); (2) Document processing queue — async pipeline with priority levels (urgent updates vs. bulk updates), deduplication, and failure retry; (3) Incremental updates — strategy for updating only changed chunks rather than re-processing entire documents; (4) Deletion handling — propagating document deletions to the vector store to prevent stale content retrieval; (5) Version control — maintaining document version history in metadata for temporal queries; (6) Quality gates — automated checks before ingestion (content length validation, format checks, duplicate detection); (7) Update monitoring — tracking time-to-indexing, ingestion failure rate, and knowledge base freshness metrics. Provide the pipeline architecture diagram description and the document change detection pseudocode.","tags":["knowledge-base","rag","data-engineering"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-48","title":"Cross-Encoder Re-Ranking Implementation","prompt":"Implement a production-ready cross-encoder re-ranking layer for a RAG system that currently uses bi-encoder retrieval. The bi-encoder retrieves the top-50 candidates quickly but ranking quality is poor because bi-encoders cannot model query-document interaction. Design and implement the re-ranking system: (1) Cross-encoder selection — compare ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2, ms-marco-electra-base, and Cohere Rerank API on accuracy vs. latency vs. cost; (2) Integration architecture — how to insert re-ranking between the retrieval step and the LLM generation step without blocking the critical path; (3) Inference optimization — batch scoring of 50 candidates, GPU vs. CPU trade-offs for this component, ONNX export for faster CPU inference; (4) Top-K selection — how to choose the final K documents to pass to the LLM after re-ranking; (5) Latency budget — the re-ranking step must add less than 300ms; calculate throughput requirements for 100 concurrent users; (6) Evaluation — measure NDCG@5 improvement from bi-encoder-only to bi-encoder + cross-encoder; (7) Complete Python implementation using sentence-transformers for local deployment with async batching. Include performance benchmarks.","tags":["rag","re-ranking","information-retrieval"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-49","title":"Prompt Template Management System","prompt":"Design a prompt template management system for an engineering organization with 15 teams maintaining 200+ prompts across production AI features. The current state: prompts are hardcoded in application code, there is no versioning, no testing, and no visibility into which prompt version is running in production. System design: (1) Template storage — schema for storing prompts with: unique ID, version (semver), status (draft/staging/production), variables/slots definition, linked model and parameters, author, changelog; (2) Variable system — templating syntax for injecting dynamic values, type validation for variables, required vs. optional slots; (3) Version control workflow — PR-based prompt changes with automated testing gate, review process, staging deployment, and production promotion; (4) SDK design — how application code retrieves and renders prompts at runtime (lazy loading, caching, hot reload); (5) A/B testing integration — built-in support for routing percentage of traffic to prompt variant B; (6) Analytics — usage tracking, cost per prompt, quality score trend per version; (7) Access control — team-level ownership, read/write permissions per prompt. Provide the template schema DDL and the SDK interface definition.","tags":["prompt-management","mlops","engineering"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-50","title":"Multimodal Document Understanding Pipeline","prompt":"Design a multimodal document understanding pipeline for a financial services firm that needs to process mixed-content documents: PDF reports containing charts, tables, handwritten annotations, and scanned images alongside text. The pipeline must extract structured data from charts (values, labels, trends), parse financial tables into machine-readable format, recognize handwritten notes, and integrate all extracted content for downstream LLM analysis. Architecture: (1) Document parsing — PDF extraction with layout preservation (using pdfplumber or pymupdf), page segmentation into text regions, image regions, and table regions; (2) Chart understanding — vision model pipeline for chart type classification, data extraction (bar values, line trends), and axis label parsing; (3) Table extraction — structured table parsing using a table detection model + cell content OCR, output as JSON with headers; (4) Handwriting recognition — HTR (Handwritten Text Recognition) model integration; (5) Content fusion — how to reassemble all extracted elements into a coherent document representation for RAG indexing; (6) Quality validation — confidence scoring per element and human review routing for low-confidence extractions; (7) Cost and latency at 10,000 documents/day. Provide the processing pipeline pseudocode and the unified document schema.","tags":["multimodal","document-processing","ocr"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-51","title":"Speculative Decoding Implementation","prompt":"Explain and implement speculative decoding to reduce LLM inference latency for a self-hosted Llama-3-70B deployment. The current P50 generation latency is 12 seconds for 500-token responses. Design the speculative decoding system: (1) Core concept — how a small draft model generates K candidate tokens that the large target model verifies in parallel, achieving near-linear speedup; (2) Draft model selection — choosing a draft model (Llama-3-8B as draft for Llama-3-70B), acceptance rate expectations, and the impact of draft quality on actual speedup; (3) Acceptance sampling algorithm — implement the speculative decoding acceptance/rejection algorithm that guarantees the output distribution matches the target model exactly; (4) Hyperparameter tuning — optimal K (draft tokens per speculation step), and how to tune K based on empirical acceptance rate; (5) Integration with vLLM — configuration for speculative decoding in vLLM, memory overhead for running both models; (6) Benchmarking — methodology for measuring wall-clock speedup under different acceptance rate conditions; (7) When not to use it — query types where speculative decoding provides little benefit. Include the acceptance sampling pseudocode and expected latency improvement calculation.","tags":["inference","optimization","deployment"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-52","title":"AI Product Roadmap Planning Framework","prompt":"You are the AI Product Manager for a mid-size B2B software company that has just completed its first AI integration (a basic GPT-4 Q&A chatbot). The executive team wants a 12-month AI product roadmap. Design a structured AI product roadmap planning framework: (1) Capability maturity assessment — score current AI capabilities across: data infrastructure, model deployment, evaluation practices, and AI literacy; (2) Opportunity mapping — framework for identifying and scoring AI feature opportunities (user pain × frequency × AI suitability × build vs. buy); (3) Build vs. buy vs. partner decision criteria — when to use foundation model APIs, when to fine-tune, when to build custom models, and when to acquire an AI startup; (4) Roadmap structure — how to organize AI initiatives into: quick wins (0-3 months), foundational capabilities (3-6 months), and strategic bets (6-12 months); (5) Dependency mapping — identify which foundational investments (data pipelines, eval frameworks, model serving) unlock multiple product features; (6) Risk-adjusted prioritization — scoring each initiative by: expected impact, implementation confidence, and strategic alignment; (7) OKR alignment — how to connect AI feature outcomes to company-level business metrics. Provide a template roadmap structure with example initiatives.","tags":["ai-product","roadmap","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-53","title":"Embedding Fine-Tuning with Contrastive Learning","prompt":"Fine-tune a text embedding model using contrastive learning to improve retrieval performance for a specialized domain (cybersecurity threat intelligence). The default embedding models perform poorly because cybersecurity terminology, CVE identifiers, and threat actor names are underrepresented in generic training data. Design the fine-tuning pipeline: (1) Training data construction — how to mine hard negatives from cybersecurity documents using BM25 top-k retrieval; generate in-batch negatives; and use GPT-4 to create synthetic query-document pairs for rare threat categories; (2) Loss function — MultipleNegativesRankingLoss vs. CosineSimilarityLoss vs. TripletLoss — select and justify the appropriate loss for this use case; (3) Model architecture — starting from BGE-M3 or E5-large, which layers to unfreeze, and learning rate layerwise decay; (4) Training configuration — batch size impact on contrastive learning quality, gradient checkpointing, mixed precision training; (5) Evaluation protocol — BEIR benchmark subset selection relevant to cybersecurity, Recall@1/5/10 on a held-out threat intelligence query set; (6) Production serving — ONNX export, dynamic batching configuration, and embedding caching strategy. Provide training configuration code using sentence-transformers.","tags":["embeddings","fine-tuning","contrastive-learning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-54","title":"LLM Judge Design and Calibration","prompt":"Design and calibrate an LLM-as-judge evaluation system for assessing customer service AI responses. The judge must evaluate responses on: helpfulness, accuracy, tone, completeness, and appropriate handling of sensitive situations. The challenge is that naive LLM judges have systematic biases (position bias, verbosity bias, self-preference bias). Design a bias-mitigated judge system: (1) Rubric design — define a 5-point scale for each dimension with specific behavioral anchors and 2 annotated examples per score level; (2) Bias mitigations — swap augmentation for position bias (evaluate A vs. B and B vs. A, average scores), explicit anti-verbosity instruction, chain-of-thought reasoning before scoring; (3) Calibration methodology — compare judge scores against human annotator scores on 500 calibration examples, calculate Spearman correlation and systematic bias per dimension; (4) Judge prompt template — complete system prompt for the judge including rubric, bias mitigations, and output format; (5) Disagreement handling — when human and LLM judge disagree by more than 1 point, what process resolves the discrepancy; (6) Ongoing calibration — how to monitor judge calibration drift in production. Include the complete judge prompt and calibration analysis methodology.","tags":["evals","llm-judge","quality-assurance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:25:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"aie-55","title":"Privacy-Preserving AI System Design","prompt":"Design a privacy-preserving AI system for a healthcare provider that needs to use LLMs to analyze patient records for clinical decision support, while complying with HIPAA and preventing PHI (Protected Health Information) from being sent to external AI APIs. Architecture: (1) PHI detection and anonymization — NLP pipeline for detecting and replacing 18 HIPAA PHI categories (names, dates, geographic data, contact info, identifiers) before sending to any external model; (2) Deployment options — fully on-premises LLM deployment vs. private cloud (Azure OpenAI with HIPAA BAA) vs. anonymize-then-use-API — trade-off analysis for each; (3) De-identification quality — evaluation methodology for measuring PHI leakage rate and clinical utility preservation after anonymization; (4) Differential privacy — where and how to apply DP to model training and fine-tuning pipelines; (5) Audit and access controls — logging all data access, model queries, and outputs with patient record linkage for audit trail; (6) Re-identification risk assessment — residual risk after anonymization and acceptable thresholds; (7) Incident response — procedure when PHI is detected in a model output or sent to an external API. Provide the PHI detection pipeline architecture and the anonymization schema.","tags":["privacy","healthcare","compliance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-56","title":"Adversarial Robustness Testing for NLP","prompt":"Design an adversarial robustness testing framework for a sentiment classification model deployed in a brand monitoring system. Brands pay for accurate real-time sentiment analysis of social media mentions, and adversarial content (deliberate obfuscation, sarcasm, unicode tricks, adversarial examples) degrades model accuracy. Testing framework: (1) Attack taxonomy — character-level attacks (typos, character substitution, zero-width characters), word-level attacks (synonym replacement, antonym insertion, negation addition), sentence-level attacks (paraphrasing, style transfer, sarcasm injection); (2) Automated attack generation — TextAttack library configuration for generating adversarial examples from the test set; (3) Evaluation metrics — accuracy drop under attack, minimum perturbation needed for misclassification, attack success rate by attack type; (4) Defense strategies — adversarial training (augment training with adversarial examples), input preprocessing (unicode normalization, spelling correction), detection-based defense (flag suspicious inputs); (5) Continuous red-team pipeline — automated weekly adversarial evaluation as part of the model monitoring workflow; (6) Prioritization — which attack types pose the most realistic business risk. Provide the TextAttack configuration and the defense evaluation methodology.","tags":["adversarial-robustness","nlp","security"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-57","title":"Stream Processing for Real-Time AI Features","prompt":"Design a stream processing architecture for real-time AI features in a social media analytics platform. The platform needs to: classify content sentiment and toxicity in real-time as posts arrive, extract named entities and trending topics, generate brief summaries for high-engagement posts, and feed results to a live dashboard with under 5-second end-to-end latency at 10,000 posts/minute peak throughput. Architecture: (1) Ingestion layer — Kafka topic design with partitioning strategy for parallel processing; (2) Stream processing framework — Flink vs. Kafka Streams vs. Spark Streaming — choose and justify for this use case; (3) AI inference integration — how to batch inference requests from the stream to maximize GPU utilization while respecting latency constraints (micro-batching strategy); (4) Model deployment — Triton Inference Server configuration for serving multiple models (classifier + NER + summarizer) with shared GPU memory; (5) Backpressure handling — what happens when inference throughput cannot keep up with ingestion rate; (6) State management — tracking entity mention counts and trend detection with windowed aggregations; (7) Output routing — fan-out to dashboard WebSocket feeds, database writes, and downstream services. Include the Kafka topic configuration and the micro-batching logic pseudocode.","tags":["streaming","real-time","mlops"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-58","title":"AI System Observability Stack","prompt":"Design a comprehensive observability stack for a production AI platform running 20 LLM-powered features. Standard application monitoring (CPU, memory, error rates) is insufficient for AI systems because the most important quality signals are in the content of inputs and outputs, not infrastructure metrics. Observability design: (1) Metrics layer — define 15 key AI-specific metrics beyond standard APM: token usage distribution, cache hit rate, model routing decisions, output length distribution, tool call success rate, context utilization percentage, and per-feature quality scores; (2) Tracing layer — LLM-aware distributed tracing that captures the full request lifecycle including prompt assembly, retrieval steps, model calls, and tool executions — include span attributes specific to LLM operations; (3) Logging layer — structured log schema for AI interactions (sanitized of PII), sampling strategy to keep storage costs manageable; (4) Quality monitoring — automated sampling pipeline that runs LLM-judge quality scoring on 1% of production traffic; (5) Alerting — alert rules for each metric with thresholds, severity, and runbook links; (6) Tooling selection — compare Langfuse, Arize Phoenix, and OpenTelemetry + Grafana for this use case. Provide the OpenTelemetry span schema for LLM operations.","tags":["observability","monitoring","mlops"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-59","title":"Hierarchical Summarization for Long Documents","prompt":"Design a hierarchical summarization pipeline for processing lengthy documents (annual reports, legal contracts, research papers averaging 80 pages) to enable accurate Q&A and key information extraction. Single-pass summarization loses too much detail; naive chunking loses cross-document coherence. Pipeline design: (1) Document segmentation — identify natural structural boundaries (chapters, sections, clauses) rather than fixed-length splits; (2) Leaf-level summarization — summarize each section with a structured template (key claims, named entities, numerical data, action items) preserving facts that are likely to be queried; (3) Hierarchical aggregation — build a summary tree from section summaries up to chapter summaries up to document summary; (4) Metadata preservation — what structured data (tables, figures, citations) to extract separately and store outside the summary hierarchy; (5) Query routing — given a user question, determine which level of the hierarchy to retrieve from, or whether to drill down to source chunks; (6) Summary quality evaluation — faithfulness score (does the summary contain only information from the source?) and coverage score (does it include the important information?); (7) Prompt templates for each summarization level. Include the hierarchical data structure schema and query routing logic.","tags":["summarization","long-context","rag"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-60","title":"Model Card and AI Documentation Standards","prompt":"You are establishing AI documentation standards for an organization deploying 12 AI models in production. Currently there is no documentation practice, creating regulatory risk and operational problems when models behave unexpectedly. Design a comprehensive documentation framework: (1) Model card template — mandatory fields: model purpose and intended use cases, out-of-scope uses, training data description, evaluation results by demographic subgroup, known limitations, bias considerations, and maintenance contacts; (2) System card extension — for AI features that combine multiple models and components: architecture diagram, data flow, external dependencies, failure modes, and human oversight points; (3) Data sheet template — for training datasets: collection methodology, demographic representation, labeling process, known quality issues, and update history; (4) Living documentation process — how model cards are updated when models are retrained, when issues are discovered, or when use cases expand; (5) Documentation review gate — checklist that must pass before a model is approved for production deployment; (6) Stakeholder-specific views — how to generate regulatory-facing, engineering-facing, and executive-facing documentation from the same source. Provide the model card template with example content for a customer churn prediction model.","tags":["documentation","governance","responsible-ai"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-61","title":"Zero-Shot vs. Few-Shot Prompting Decision Guide","prompt":"Design a systematic framework for deciding between zero-shot, one-shot, and few-shot prompting across different task types and model capabilities. The engineering team is wasting time adding unnecessary examples or, conversely, underperforming on tasks where examples would help. Framework: (1) Task taxonomy — classify tasks by: structure complexity (simple classification vs. multi-step generation), output format strictness (free text vs. rigid JSON schema), domain specificity (general knowledge vs. specialized domain), and edge case density; (2) Model capability baseline — establish zero-shot performance baseline and the minimum acceptable score; (3) Example selection criteria — when few-shot examples help: diverse edge case coverage, output format demonstration, reasoning pattern illustration; when they hurt: when examples bias the model toward a narrow style; (4) Example count optimization — empirical method for finding the minimum number of examples for marginal gain; (5) Dynamic few-shot selection — retrieve semantically similar examples at runtime rather than static examples; (6) Cost-quality trade-off — tokens consumed by examples vs. quality improvement; (7) Task-by-task recommendation table covering 10 common AI tasks. Include example prompts demonstrating the same task with zero-shot, one-shot, and three-shot approaches.","tags":["prompt-engineering","few-shot","optimization"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-62","title":"AI-Assisted Code Review System","prompt":"Design an AI-assisted code review system that integrates into a GitHub pull request workflow for a 50-engineer engineering team. The system must provide automated feedback on: security vulnerabilities, performance anti-patterns, test coverage gaps, documentation quality, and code style violations — while avoiding unhelpful nitpicking that annoys developers. System design: (1) Trigger and scope — which PRs get AI review (size thresholds, file type filters, opt-out mechanisms); (2) Context assembly — how to provide the LLM with relevant context: the diff, surrounding file context, related test files, and project-specific coding standards; (3) Prompt design — system prompt that calibrates the review tone (collegial, not authoritative), focuses on high-value feedback, and suppresses low-signal comments; (4) Comment categorization — blocking issues (security, correctness) vs. suggestions (style, performance) vs. questions (clarification needed); (5) False positive management — feedback mechanism and automated suppression of repeated false positives; (6) Security analysis depth — when to trigger a deeper security-focused analysis vs. standard review; (7) Integration architecture — GitHub Actions workflow, comment formatting, and diff-aware comment placement. Provide the system prompt and the context assembly algorithm.","tags":["code-review","developer-tools","agents"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-63","title":"Continual Learning Strategy for Production Models","prompt":"Design a continual learning strategy for a production NLP model that classifies customer support tickets into 50 categories. New product launches create new categories, customer language evolves, and the model's accuracy degrades 3-5% every quarter without retraining. Strategy: (1) Data flywheel design — pipeline for capturing production predictions, routing low-confidence examples for human labeling, and accumulating a high-quality training corpus; (2) Catastrophic forgetting mitigation — elastic weight consolidation (EWC), experience replay, and progressive neural networks — compare and recommend for this use case; (3) New class addition — how to add a new ticket category without retraining the entire model from scratch; (4) Retraining trigger criteria — data volume thresholds, accuracy degradation alerts, and concept drift detection that trigger a retraining run; (5) Training pipeline — incremental fine-tuning configuration (learning rate, epochs, regularization) that updates the model on new data without forgetting old categories; (6) Validation before deployment — accuracy regression test on historical categories plus new category accuracy; (7) Rollout strategy — A/B test new model against production model with automatic rollback. Provide the concept drift detection algorithm and the retraining trigger configuration.","tags":["continual-learning","mlops","production"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-64","title":"HyDE Query Expansion for RAG","prompt":"Implement Hypothetical Document Embedding (HyDE) query expansion to improve retrieval quality in a RAG system for a scientific literature search engine. Standard vector search struggles with queries that use different vocabulary than the papers (e.g., a user asks in plain English about a concept described in highly technical terms in the papers). HyDE implementation: (1) Core mechanism — prompt the LLM to generate a hypothetical document that would answer the query, then embed the hypothetical document instead of (or in addition to) the original query; (2) Generation prompt design — write the HyDE generation prompt that produces a plausible, content-dense hypothetical document in the style of the target corpus (scientific abstracts); (3) Embedding strategy — embed the hypothetical document alone vs. average the query and hypothetical document embeddings — provide evaluation methodology to choose; (4) Multiple hypothetical documents — generate 3-5 hypothetical documents and average their embeddings to reduce variance; (5) Computational cost — HyDE adds one LLM call per query; calculate latency and cost impact and when to apply it selectively; (6) Evaluation — measure Recall@10 improvement on a scientific query benchmark; (7) Failure cases — query types where HyDE performs worse than direct embedding. Provide the complete HyDE implementation in Python.","tags":["rag","query-expansion","hyde"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-65","title":"AI Governance Maturity Model","prompt":"Design an AI Governance Maturity Model for enterprises to assess and improve their AI governance practices. The model should help organizations progress from ad-hoc AI deployment to fully governed, auditable AI operations. Define five maturity levels: Level 1 (Ad-hoc): no formal AI governance; Level 2 (Developing): basic policies exist; Level 3 (Defined): documented processes consistently followed; Level 4 (Managed): quantitative governance metrics tracked; Level 5 (Optimizing): continuous improvement with feedback loops. For each level, define: (1) Governance capabilities present — policy documentation, risk assessment, human oversight, audit trails, bias testing, vendor management; (2) Organizational structure — who owns AI governance at this level; (3) Tooling and infrastructure — what technical capabilities support governance; (4) Assessment criteria — specific observable evidence that confirms a level; (5) Advancement roadmap — the 3-5 key initiatives needed to advance to the next level; (6) Risk exposure — regulatory and reputational risks associated with operating at each level. Include a self-assessment questionnaire (30 questions across 6 governance domains) and a scoring methodology that maps to maturity levels.","tags":["governance","ai-ethics","maturity-model"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-66","title":"Batch Inference Pipeline Optimization","prompt":"Optimize a batch inference pipeline that processes 2 million customer records nightly through an LLM to generate personalized product recommendations. The current pipeline takes 14 hours and must complete within 6 hours to meet the downstream data warehouse loading window. Optimization strategy: (1) Throughput analysis — calculate current tokens/second, identify the bottleneck (GPU compute, CPU preprocessing, network I/O, API rate limits); (2) Parallelization — multi-threaded and async request patterns, optimal concurrency level calculation based on rate limits and GPU memory; (3) Request batching — dynamic batching configuration to maximize GPU utilization while respecting memory constraints; (4) Prompt optimization — reduce average prompt length by 30% through template compression to increase throughput; (5) Caching — identify records with identical or near-identical contexts that can share cached prefix computations; (6) Priority queue — process highest-value customers first so the most important outputs are ready even if full completion is delayed; (7) Infrastructure scaling — cost-benefit of spinning up additional GPU instances for the nightly window vs. optimizing the single-instance pipeline; (8) Failure handling — checkpoint and resume strategy if the pipeline fails mid-run. Provide the async batching implementation and throughput calculation.","tags":["batch-inference","optimization","mlops"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-67","title":"Prompt Chaining for Complex Document Analysis","prompt":"[Paste your current prompt or system instruction]\n\nYou are redesigning the prompt above as a multi-step prompt chain to improve quality and reliability on complex document analysis tasks. Single-prompt approaches fail because they ask the model to do too many things simultaneously (read, extract, reason, format). Redesign as a chain: (1) Chain decomposition — break the single prompt into 3-5 sequential steps, each with a single clear objective; explain the reasoning for each boundary; (2) Step design — for each step in the chain: write the complete prompt, specify the expected output format, and explain how the output feeds into the next step; (3) Error handling — what to do if an intermediate step produces low-quality or malformed output (retry, fallback, human escalation); (4) Context propagation — specify exactly what information passes between steps and how to format handover data; (5) Parallelization opportunities — identify any steps that can run in parallel to reduce total latency; (6) Quality checkpoints — where to insert automated validation between steps; (7) Cost analysis — total token cost for the chain vs. the single-prompt approach, justified by the quality improvement. Provide the complete chain implementation as a Python function with step descriptions.","tags":["prompt-chaining","document-analysis","prompt-engineering"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-68","title":"AI-Powered Data Extraction Pipeline","prompt":"Design an AI-powered structured data extraction pipeline that processes unstructured business documents (invoices, contracts, purchase orders, delivery notes) and populates a structured database. The pipeline must handle varied document layouts, handwritten fields, poor scan quality, and 15 different document types with different schemas. Pipeline design: (1) Document classification — model to identify document type from the first page, confidence threshold for routing to manual review; (2) Schema-aware extraction — per-document-type extraction prompts with the target JSON schema embedded, field descriptions, and validation rules; (3) Multi-page handling — strategy for extracting data that spans multiple pages (line items in a 10-page invoice); (4) Confidence scoring — field-level confidence scores and the threshold below which a field is flagged for human verification; (5) Validation layer — cross-field validation rules (total = sum of line items, dates are chronologically consistent), with automatic correction prompting for violations; (6) Human-in-the-loop UI — design spec for the review interface showing low-confidence fields highlighted for human correction; (7) Continuous improvement — how human corrections feed back into prompt improvement and fine-tuning. Include the extraction prompt template and the validation rule schema.","tags":["data-extraction","document-processing","structured-output"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-69","title":"LLM Security Threat Modeling","prompt":"Conduct a comprehensive security threat model for an LLM-powered internal enterprise assistant that has access to: company Slack/Teams messages (read), Confluence wiki (read/write), Jira tickets (read/write/create), and GitHub repositories (read, PR creation). The assistant is accessed by 500 employees. Threat model using STRIDE methodology: (1) Spoofing — identity spoofing attacks (impersonating another employee to access their data, social engineering the assistant to act as someone else); (2) Tampering — indirect prompt injection via malicious content in Confluence pages or Slack messages that the assistant reads; (3) Repudiation — audit trail gaps that prevent attributing actions to specific users; (4) Information disclosure — cross-user data leakage, extraction of confidential information via crafted queries; (5) Denial of service — resource exhaustion attacks via context flooding, malicious loops; (6) Elevation of privilege — using the assistant to perform actions beyond the querying user's permission level. For each threat: describe the attack vector, likelihood, impact, and specific mitigation. Conclude with a security architecture diagram description and a prioritized security backlog.","tags":["security","threat-modeling","agents"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-70","title":"Evaluation-Driven Prompt Development","prompt":"[Describe your AI use case and constraints]\n\nYou are applying evaluation-driven development to build and iteratively improve a prompt for the use case described above. This methodology treats prompt development like test-driven development: define measurable success criteria first, then develop the prompt to meet them. Process: (1) Success criteria definition — define 5-8 measurable evaluation criteria specific to this use case, with target scores and minimum acceptable scores for each; (2) Evaluation dataset — specify how to construct a 50-100 example evaluation set: what input types to include, how to create ground truth, how to ensure coverage of edge cases and failure modes; (3) Baseline measurement — measure a naive first-draft prompt against all criteria to establish the starting point; (4) Hypothesis-driven iteration — for each iteration, state the hypothesis (adding X to the prompt will improve criterion Y), implement the change, measure the impact; (5) Ablation analysis — systematically remove components of the best-performing prompt to understand which elements are load-bearing; (6) Regression prevention — define which criteria are must-not-regress and the gate for approving a new prompt version; (7) Documentation — how to document the development history so future developers understand why the prompt is structured as it is. Provide the evaluation dataset schema and the iteration tracking template.","tags":["evals","prompt-engineering","development-process"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-71","title":"Open-Source LLM Deployment Stack","prompt":"Design a complete open-source LLM deployment stack for a company that wants to run its own LLMs to avoid data privacy concerns and API costs. The target workload: Llama-3-70B-Instruct serving an internal knowledge management assistant with 200 concurrent users, 8-hour operating window, on 4x NVIDIA A100 80GB GPUs. Stack selection and configuration: (1) Inference server — compare vLLM, TGI (Text Generation Inference), and Ollama for this workload; select and justify; (2) Model loading — tensor parallelism configuration for 4-GPU setup, pipeline parallelism option; (3) Quantization — INT4 AWQ quantization to fit within GPU memory budget with quality validation; (4) API layer — OpenAI-compatible API endpoint configuration so existing code works without modification; (5) Load balancing — multi-instance load balancer setup for high availability; (6) Monitoring — Prometheus metrics export, Grafana dashboard for GPU utilization, queue depth, and tokens/second; (7) Deployment — Docker Compose and Kubernetes Helm chart structure; (8) Security — network isolation, authentication layer, audit logging. Provide the vLLM launch configuration, Docker Compose file structure, and expected throughput calculation.","tags":["open-source","deployment","infrastructure"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-72","title":"Debate Prompting for High-Stakes Decisions","prompt":"Design a debate prompting architecture for a high-stakes AI decision support system used by a hospital ethics committee to evaluate complex clinical scenarios. Single-model responses are insufficient because they may miss important counterarguments and present false certainty. Architecture: (1) Debate structure — define the roles: Advocate (argues for a proposed action), Devil's Advocate (argues against), Neutral Synthesizer (summarizes the strongest arguments from both sides); (2) Prompt design — complete system prompts for each role, ensuring each role genuinely argues its position rather than hedging; (3) Debate rounds — how many exchange rounds produce the best synthesis without diminishing returns; (4) Synthesis quality — prompt for the final synthesis that explicitly identifies: strongest pro-argument, strongest counter-argument, unresolved uncertainties, and recommended decision with confidence level; (5) Bias detection — how to detect when a debate collapses because both sides converge prematurely; (6) Use case applicability — clinical scenarios where debate prompting adds value vs. where it is unnecessary overhead; (7) Evaluation — human expert comparison to assess whether debate outputs contain more complete reasoning than single-pass responses. Provide all three role prompts and a worked example trace.","tags":["prompt-engineering","reasoning","debate-prompting"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-73","title":"AI Feature A/B Testing Framework","prompt":"Design a rigorous A/B testing framework specifically for AI features, addressing the unique challenges that AI introduces compared to traditional product A/B tests. AI-specific challenges: output variability (same input can produce different outputs), quality measurement (no binary pass/fail metric), long-tail failure modes, and the difficulty of isolating prompt changes from model behavior changes. Framework: (1) Experiment design — how to randomize users to control and treatment groups for an AI feature, maintaining consistent assignment across sessions; (2) Sample size calculation — power analysis for AI quality metrics (which have higher variance than click metrics), minimum detectable effect sizes; (3) Metrics hierarchy — primary metric (e.g., user acceptance rate), guardrail metrics (safety, cost), and diagnostic metrics (output length, latency); (4) Quality evaluation at scale — hybrid approach combining automated metrics (LLM judge) and sampled human evaluation; (5) Interaction effects — how to detect when the AI treatment changes behavior for only a subset of query types; (6) Novelty effect — controlling for users behaving differently simply because something is new; (7) Shipping criteria — statistical significance requirements, practical significance thresholds, and the approval process. Provide the experiment configuration schema and analysis methodology.","tags":["a-b-testing","experimentation","ai-product"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-74","title":"Prompt Security for Multi-User Applications","prompt":"Design a prompt security architecture for a multi-user AI application where different users have different permission levels and must not be able to access each other's data or escalate their own privileges. The application: enterprise SaaS with admin, manager, and employee roles; each role has different allowed actions and data access scopes; user-provided input is incorporated into prompts that access a shared knowledge base. Security architecture: (1) Privilege separation — how to structurally prevent user-supplied content from being interpreted as system instructions; using XML tags, clear delimiters, and content sanitization; (2) Role encoding — how to encode user role and permissions into the system prompt in a tamper-resistant way; (3) Data isolation enforcement — prompt instructions that prevent the LLM from revealing documents outside the user's permission scope; (4) Privilege escalation defenses — specific prompt patterns that prevent users from claiming to be admins, invoking emergency modes, or using roleplay to bypass restrictions; (5) Input validation — pre-processing checks on user input before it enters the prompt; (6) Canary token deployment — embedding role-specific tokens to detect prompt extraction attempts; (7) Penetration test suite — 20 specific attacks against this architecture with expected outcomes. Provide the secure prompt template and the input sanitization pseudocode.","tags":["security","prompt-engineering","multi-user"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-75","title":"Vector Search Performance Tuning","prompt":"Tune the vector search performance of a Qdrant deployment serving a customer-facing semantic search feature. Current performance: 250ms P95 latency with 10M vectors, target is 80ms P95. Throughput: 200 queries per second peak. Systematic performance tuning guide: (1) HNSW index parameter tuning — ef_construction (build quality vs. index time), m (connectivity vs. memory), ef (search quality vs. latency) — provide tuning methodology with expected latency/recall trade-offs for each parameter change; (2) Payload indexing — identify which metadata filters can be pre-indexed to enable pre-filtering vs. post-filtering, and the performance impact; (3) Quantization — scalar quantization (INT8) vs. product quantization vs. binary quantization — trade-offs for this workload; (4) Collection sharding — horizontal sharding strategy for 10M vectors across multiple nodes; (5) Query-time parameters — ef search parameter tuning per query type; (6) Caching — application-level semantic cache for popular queries, Qdrant's internal cache configuration; (7) Hardware optimization — optimal CPU vs. GPU placement for this vector search workload; (8) Benchmarking methodology — using qdrant-client benchmarking tools to measure improvements. Provide the HNSW tuning experiments table and expected latency improvements.","tags":["vector-db","performance","search"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-76","title":"AI-Assisted Requirements Engineering","prompt":"Design an AI-assisted requirements engineering workflow for a product team building a complex B2B software feature. Traditional requirements gathering is slow, inconsistent, and often misses edge cases. The AI assistant should help transform raw stakeholder input into structured, testable requirements. Workflow design: (1) Stakeholder interview support — prompts that help extract requirements from vague stakeholder statements, generating clarifying questions, identifying implicit assumptions, and surfacing conflicting requirements; (2) Requirements structuring — convert raw interview notes into structured user stories with acceptance criteria (Given/When/Then format); (3) Completeness analysis — prompt that analyzes a requirements set and identifies: missing edge cases, undefined error states, unstated assumptions, and gaps in non-functional requirements; (4) Conflict detection — identify contradictory requirements across different stakeholder inputs; (5) Test case generation — automatically generate test scenarios from acceptance criteria; (6) Requirements traceability — generate traceability matrix linking requirements to business goals; (7) Change impact analysis — when a requirement changes, identify all downstream affected requirements and test cases. Provide the stakeholder interview prompt, requirements structuring template, and the completeness analysis prompt.","tags":["requirements","ai-product","developer-tools"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-77","title":"Federated Learning for Privacy-Preserving AI","prompt":"Design a federated learning architecture for a healthcare consortium of 15 hospitals that want to jointly train a clinical NLP model (ICD code prediction from clinical notes) without sharing patient data across institutions. Each hospital has 50,000-200,000 training examples. Architecture: (1) FL framework selection — compare Flower (flwr), PySyft, and TensorFlow Federated for this use case; (2) Aggregation strategy — FedAvg vs. FedProx (for heterogeneous data) vs. FedNova — select based on expected data heterogeneity across hospitals; (3) Communication protocol — how model updates are transmitted securely, update compression techniques (sparsification, quantization) to reduce communication overhead; (4) Privacy guarantees — differential privacy integration (DP-SGD) with privacy budget allocation across rounds; (5) Byzantine robustness — strategies to detect and exclude malicious or low-quality updates from poorly performing hospitals; (6) Convergence monitoring — metrics to track federated training convergence vs. centralized training baseline; (7) Infrastructure — coordinator server setup, client deployment at each hospital, and the orchestration protocol for training rounds. Include the FedAvg aggregation pseudocode and differential privacy configuration.","tags":["federated-learning","privacy","healthcare"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-78","title":"Instruction Tuning Dataset Quality Framework","prompt":"Design a quality assessment and improvement framework for instruction tuning datasets. Poor dataset quality is the leading cause of fine-tuned model failures, yet most teams use ad-hoc quality checks. Framework: (1) Quality dimensions — define 7 measurable quality dimensions for instruction-response pairs: instruction clarity, response correctness, instruction-following adherence, response completeness, format consistency, diversity (avoiding near-duplicates), and safety; (2) Automated scoring — implement automated checkers for each dimension: length distribution analysis, near-duplicate detection (MinHash LSH), format compliance regex, and a lightweight quality classifier; (3) LLM-based quality scoring — design a judge prompt that evaluates response quality against the instruction, with calibration methodology; (4) Active learning for annotation — how to prioritize which examples need human review based on automated uncertainty signals; (5) Dataset contamination detection — check for overlap with popular benchmarks (MMLU, HellaSwag) to prevent evaluation contamination; (6) Diversity analysis — embedding-space analysis to identify clusters of near-duplicate instructions and pruning strategy; (7) Quality improvement pipeline — workflow for flagging low-quality examples, routing to human annotators, and replacing poor examples. Provide the quality scoring rubric and deduplication algorithm.","tags":["fine-tuning","data-quality","instruction-tuning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:25:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"aie-79","title":"AI Integration Testing Strategy","prompt":"Design a comprehensive integration testing strategy for an AI-powered application where LLM outputs feed into downstream systems (database writes, API calls, email triggers, report generation). Testing is harder than standard software because outputs are non-deterministic and quality is subjective. Strategy: (1) Test environment design — sandboxed AI integration test environment that uses the real model but isolated infrastructure; (2) Determinism management — how to make tests reliable despite LLM non-determinism (temperature=0, deterministic seed where supported, semantic assertion instead of exact match); (3) Contract testing — define contracts between the AI component and downstream consumers specifying: output schema requirements, field presence guarantees, and value constraints; (4) Semantic assertions — testing framework that validates output meaning rather than exact text (embedding similarity threshold, LLM judge assertions, regex pattern matching for structured fields); (5) Failure injection — test scenarios where the LLM returns malformed output, truncated responses, or schema violations; (6) End-to-end test suite — 30 critical user journey tests covering happy paths and edge cases; (7) CI pipeline integration — how to run AI integration tests efficiently in CI without excessive cost or latency. Provide the semantic assertion framework and contract definition schema.","tags":["testing","integration-testing","engineering"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-80","title":"Conversational AI Dialogue Management","prompt":"Design the dialogue management system for a complex conversational AI assistant that handles multi-turn interactions for an insurance claims process. Users go through a 15-step claims submission workflow over multiple conversation turns, and the assistant must maintain state, handle interruptions, clarify ambiguities, and guide users back on track. Dialogue design: (1) State machine — define the claims workflow as a state machine with states, transitions, and transition conditions; specify how the LLM interacts with this state machine; (2) Slot filling — design the slot schema for all required claim information, required vs. optional slots, slot validation rules; (3) Context management — conversation history compression strategy to maintain relevant context without hitting token limits across long multi-turn conversations; (4) Interruption handling — when a user goes off-topic mid-workflow, how the assistant acknowledges the digression and guides back; (5) Clarification strategy — when and how to ask clarifying questions without annoying users; criteria for accepting ambiguous answers vs. asking for confirmation; (6) Error recovery — when the user provides invalid information, the correction dialogue pattern; (7) Completion validation — before finalizing a claim, a review step that confirms all slots are filled correctly. Provide the state machine definition and slot schema.","tags":["conversational-ai","dialogue-management","agents"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-81","title":"Graph RAG Architecture","prompt":"Design a GraphRAG implementation for a knowledge management system at a consulting firm where the most valuable information lies in the relationships between concepts, projects, clients, and methodologies — not in individual documents. Standard RAG retrieves relevant chunks but misses relational context. GraphRAG design: (1) Knowledge graph construction — entity extraction pipeline (project names, client names, methodology names, industry terms), relationship type taxonomy (used-in, developed-by, applicable-to, superseded-by), and graph storage (Neo4j); (2) Community detection — apply graph community detection (Louvain algorithm) to identify clusters of related concepts, generate community summaries; (3) Global search — query strategy that uses community summaries to answer broad synthesis questions spanning multiple documents; (4) Local search — query strategy combining vector search with graph neighborhood expansion for specific entity queries; (5) Query routing — classifier to route queries to global vs. local search strategies; (6) Response generation — how community summaries and local graph context are assembled into the LLM context; (7) Evaluation — metrics for measuring improvement over standard RAG on relational queries. Implement using the Microsoft GraphRAG framework with custom modifications for the consulting domain.","tags":["graph-rag","knowledge-graph","rag"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:40:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"aie-82","title":"LLM Output Caching Strategy","prompt":"Design a multi-layer output caching strategy for an LLM API serving layer that handles 1 million queries per day, with an estimated 25% of queries being semantically equivalent to previous queries. Caching has the potential to reduce both cost and latency significantly. Caching architecture: (1) Exact match cache — Redis-based exact string match cache with TTL policy; hit rate analysis for exact matches in typical query distributions; (2) Semantic cache — embedding-based similarity cache using cosine similarity threshold to match near-duplicate queries; Faiss index for fast nearest neighbor lookup; threshold calibration to balance precision vs. recall; (3) Prompt prefix cache — leverage provider-level prompt caching (OpenAI, Anthropic) for static system prompt prefixes; calculate expected savings; (4) Invalidation strategy — when cached responses become stale (knowledge base updates, model version changes, time-based expiry); (5) Cache warming — pre-populate cache with predicted high-frequency queries during off-peak hours; (6) Privacy considerations — ensuring cached responses from User A cannot be returned to User B; tenant isolation in the cache layer; (7) Metrics — cache hit rate tracking, cost savings calculation, latency improvement by cache tier. Provide the semantic caching implementation with threshold selection methodology.","tags":["caching","optimization","architecture"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-83","title":"AI Model Versioning and Reproducibility","prompt":"Design a model versioning and reproducibility system for an ML team that trains and deploys models monthly. Currently, reproducing a specific model version requires tribal knowledge and often fails because data, code, and environment have changed. System design: (1) Artifact versioning — what to version and store: model weights, training code (git SHA), dataset version (data hash or DVC pointer), hyperparameters, environment (Docker image digest), and evaluation results; (2) Experiment tracking — MLflow or Weights & Biases setup with mandatory logging of all reproducibility artifacts; standardized experiment naming convention; (3) Dataset versioning — DVC (Data Version Control) integration for versioning large training datasets with S3 backend; (4) Environment reproducibility — Docker image pinning strategy, dependency lock files (pip-tools, conda-lock); (5) Reproducibility verification — automated test that checks whether re-running training from logged artifacts produces a model within N% of original metric; (6) Model lineage — tracing a production model back to its training data, code, and hyperparameters via a lineage graph; (7) Regulatory requirements — audit trail requirements for model versioning in regulated industries (finance, healthcare). Provide the MLflow logging schema and the reproducibility verification pipeline.","tags":["mlops","versioning","reproducibility"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-84","title":"Prompt Personalization Architecture","prompt":"Design a prompt personalization system that dynamically adapts LLM responses based on individual user profiles for an AI tutoring application. Different students have different learning levels (beginner/intermediate/advanced), learning styles (visual/verbal/example-based), pace preferences, and prior knowledge gaps. Personalization architecture: (1) User profile schema — what attributes to track: subject knowledge level per topic, learning style preference, vocabulary level, preferred explanation length, past interaction history summaries, and topics where the student has shown confusion; (2) Profile construction — how to infer and update profile attributes from interaction signals (questions asked, follow-up patterns, explicit feedback); (3) Dynamic prompt assembly — how to inject personalization parameters into the system prompt without excessive token overhead; template design for level-adaptive explanations; (4) A/B testing personalization — how to measure whether personalized responses improve learning outcomes vs. generic responses; (5) Cold start — initial experience for new users before profile data is available; (6) Privacy — user consent for profiling, data minimization, and deletion workflows; (7) Personalization limits — topics where personalization should be suppressed (safety information, factual corrections). Provide the user profile schema and the dynamic prompt assembly function.","tags":["personalization","prompt-engineering","edtech"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-85","title":"Multimodal Embedding Space Analysis","prompt":"Design a methodology for analyzing and debugging a multimodal embedding space that jointly embeds text and images for a visual product search system. Users search with text queries ('red leather handbag with gold clasp') and the system retrieves matching product images. The system is producing poor results for certain query types. Debugging methodology: (1) Embedding space visualization — UMAP dimensionality reduction for 10,000 sample embeddings with color coding by modality (text/image) and product category; (2) Modality alignment analysis — calculate the average cosine similarity between text descriptions and their corresponding product images (alignment score), identify product categories with poor alignment; (3) Query-image similarity distribution — histogram of similarity scores for relevant vs. irrelevant results, identify the optimal threshold; (4) Hard negative analysis — for failed queries, retrieve the top-5 incorrect results and analyze what makes them confusable; (5) Domain gap assessment — identify text vocabulary or visual concepts underrepresented in the training distribution; (6) Fine-tuning diagnosis — determine whether poor performance requires: more training data, different loss function, data augmentation, or architectural changes; (7) Evaluation benchmark — construct a text-to-image retrieval benchmark (R@1, R@5, R@10) specific to the product catalog. Provide the analysis pipeline code structure.","tags":["embeddings","multimodal","debugging"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-86","title":"AI Safety Testing for Autonomous Agents","prompt":"Design a comprehensive safety testing protocol for an autonomous AI agent before its production deployment. The agent can browse the web, execute code, send emails, and manage files on behalf of users. Safety testing must be conducted without causing real-world harm. Testing protocol: (1) Capability assessment — map all actions the agent can take, categorize by reversibility (reversible vs. permanent), scope (local vs. external), and blast radius (affects 1 vs. many); (2) Boundary testing — systematic tests for each action category verifying the agent respects stated limits (does not send emails when not authorized, does not access files outside the designated folder); (3) Instruction conflict testing — present the agent with conflicting instructions (user says do X, safety policy says don't) and verify correct resolution; (4) Scope creep testing — give the agent a bounded task and verify it does not take unrequested actions on the side; (5) Manipulation resistance — attempt to convince the agent to take unsafe actions through social engineering, fictional framing, and authority claims; (6) Loop detection — tests that verify the agent terminates rather than looping infinitely when stuck; (7) Failure mode documentation — for each identified unsafe behavior, document: trigger, impact, and mitigation. Provide the test case library structure and the safety score rubric.","tags":["ai-safety","agents","testing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:05:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"aie-87","title":"Semantic Layer for Business Intelligence AI","prompt":"Design a semantic layer that enables an AI natural language to SQL system to accurately answer complex business intelligence questions for a retail analytics platform. The challenge: raw database schemas use cryptic column names, complex join paths, and business logic embedded in SQL that the LLM doesn't know. Semantic layer design: (1) Schema documentation — how to document tables, columns, relationships, and business definitions in a format the LLM can understand and reason over; (2) Business metric definitions — formal definitions of calculated metrics (same-store sales growth, customer lifetime value, churn rate) including the exact SQL logic; (3) Context injection strategy — how to select and inject the relevant subset of the semantic layer for a given query (full schema is too large for context); (4) Ambiguity resolution — how to handle ambiguous terms ('sales' could mean gross revenue, net revenue, or units sold) and ask clarifying questions; (5) Query validation — automated validation that generated SQL is syntactically correct, references only existing tables/columns, and matches the user's stated intent; (6) Explanation generation — alongside the SQL, generate a plain-English explanation of what the query does; (7) Sample queries and training data — how to build a few-shot library of question-SQL pairs. Provide the semantic layer schema and the context selection algorithm.","tags":["text-to-sql","business-intelligence","nlp"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-88","title":"PEFT Method Comparison and Selection","prompt":"You are selecting a Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) method for adapting a 13B language model to a specialized scientific writing assistant task. The team has limited GPU resources (2x A100 40GB), 8,000 training examples, and needs to deploy the adapted model alongside the base model to support multiple domain-specific variants. Compare five PEFT methods in depth: (1) LoRA — low-rank decomposition of weight updates, memory efficiency, rank selection guide, target module selection; (2) QLoRA — combines LoRA with 4-bit quantization, memory savings vs. full LoRA, quality trade-offs; (3) Prefix Tuning — prepend trainable tokens to each layer's key/value matrices, strengths for generation tasks; (4) Prompt Tuning — task-specific soft prompts, minimum viable training data, inference efficiency; (5) IA3 — fewer parameters than LoRA via rescaling activations, training speed comparison. Evaluation across: memory footprint during training, inference overhead, number of trainable parameters, performance on scientific writing benchmarks, and ease of switching between domain adapters at inference time. Recommend the best method for the stated constraints and provide the complete training configuration.","tags":["peft","fine-tuning","lora"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-89","title":"AI Content Moderation Pipeline","prompt":"Design a multi-layer AI content moderation pipeline for a user-generated content platform with 500,000 daily posts. The platform needs to detect and handle: illegal content, targeted harassment, misinformation, spam, and sexually explicit material. A purely rule-based system has too many false positives; a purely LLM-based system is too slow and expensive at scale. Pipeline design: (1) Tier 1 — fast classifier (fine-tuned BERT or similar) that processes every post in < 20ms; outputs: block, pass, escalate; (2) Tier 2 — medium classifier invoked for escalated posts (10% of volume); more nuanced categories and confidence scores; (3) Tier 3 — LLM-based deep analysis for high-ambiguity cases (1% of volume) and appeals; produces explanation and decision; (4) Human review queue — posts that fail automated confidence thresholds or are disputed by users; prioritization algorithm; (5) Context awareness — how to incorporate: user history, post context (thread, community), and multimedia content; (6) Appeals process — AI-assisted appeals review with explainability; (7) Bias monitoring — demographic parity analysis of moderation decisions; audit for over-moderation of specific communities. Include the tier routing logic and the LLM deep analysis prompt.","tags":["content-moderation","ai-safety","nlp"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-90","title":"AI System Failure Mode Analysis","prompt":"Conduct a systematic failure mode and effects analysis (FMEA) for an AI-powered autonomous expense approval system that processes 10,000 expense reports monthly. The system reviews receipts (image), expense descriptions, and policy rules to auto-approve or flag for human review. FMEA methodology for AI systems: (1) Failure mode taxonomy — categorize AI-specific failure modes: model errors (false approve, false reject), input distribution shift (new expense types), adversarial inputs (manipulated receipts), dependency failures (OCR service down, policy database stale); (2) Effects analysis — for each failure mode, document: downstream effect, who is affected, and financial/reputational impact; (3) Occurrence and detection ratings — how frequently each failure mode occurs and how easily it is detected before causing harm; (4) Risk priority scoring — RPN = Severity × Occurrence × Detection; prioritize by RPN; (5) Control measures — current controls for each failure mode and gaps; (6) Corrective actions — specific changes to the AI system, monitoring, and process to reduce the top 5 highest-RPN failure modes; (7) Residual risk — remaining risk after corrective actions and acceptance criteria. Provide the complete FMEA table with 15 failure modes and the monitoring dashboard for top risks.","tags":["risk-analysis","ai-safety","mlops"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-91","title":"Transformer Architecture Deep Dive","prompt":"Provide an engineer-oriented deep dive into the transformer architecture focused on practical implications for LLM deployment and fine-tuning decisions. Cover: (1) Attention mechanism — multi-head self-attention mathematical formulation, why multiple heads capture different relationship types, the O(n²) complexity problem and why it matters for long context; (2) KV cache — what the key-value cache stores, how it reduces autoregressive generation cost from O(n²) to O(n), memory footprint at different context lengths; (3) Positional encoding — absolute vs. rotary (RoPE) vs. ALiBi encodings, and why RoPE enables better length generalization; (4) Layer normalization — Pre-LN vs. Post-LN trade-offs and their impact on training stability; (5) MoE (Mixture of Experts) — how sparse expert routing works, the capacity factor, and implications for inference infrastructure; (6) Flash Attention — why it reduces memory from O(n²) to O(n) through tiling, when to enable it, and expected speedup; (7) Practical implications — for each architectural component, state one concrete deployment or fine-tuning decision it should inform. Include a diagram description showing data flow through a single transformer block.","tags":["transformers","deep-learning","architecture"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-92","title":"AI-Powered Search Ranking System","prompt":"Design an AI-powered search ranking system for a large e-commerce platform (50 million products) that replaces the current BM25-based keyword search with a learning-to-rank system augmented by LLM query understanding. The system must handle 5,000 queries per second at P95 latency under 150ms. Architecture: (1) Query understanding — LLM-powered query analysis that extracts: search intent (navigational/informational/transactional), product attributes (category, price range, brand preferences inferred from query), and query expansion terms; lightweight deployment for P50 < 20ms; (2) Candidate retrieval — two-stage: fast ANN vector search for semantic candidates + BM25 for keyword precision candidates, merging strategy; (3) Feature engineering — query-document features (semantic similarity, keyword overlap, title match score), product features (historical CTR, conversion rate, freshness, inventory), and contextual features (user session, device, time of day); (4) Learning-to-rank model — LambdaMART vs. neural ranker trade-offs for this latency budget; training data from click logs (DCG-based labels); (5) A/B test design — measuring search quality improvement (NDCG, revenue per search); (6) Cold start — handling new products with no click history. Include the query understanding prompt and the feature schema.","tags":["search-ranking","recommendation","architecture"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-93","title":"Responsible Disclosure for AI Vulnerabilities","prompt":"Design a responsible disclosure program for AI vulnerabilities at a company operating LLM-powered products. Traditional software vulnerability disclosure frameworks (CVE, bug bounties) do not adequately cover AI-specific vulnerabilities like prompt injection, training data extraction, model inversion, and harmful output generation. Program design: (1) AI vulnerability taxonomy — define categories with severity levels: prompt injection (critical), training data memorization (high), behavioral manipulation (medium-high), hallucination exploitation (medium), model bias exploitation (medium); (2) Disclosure policy — public policy document covering: how to report, what information to include, response time commitments (triage: 48h, remediation: 30/90/180 days by severity), safe harbor language for good-faith researchers; (3) Triage process — internal workflow for receiving, validating, and prioritizing AI vulnerability reports; (4) Remediation categories — for each vulnerability type, describe the typical remediation approach (prompt hardening, fine-tuning, architectural change, output filtering); (5) Communication — coordinated disclosure timeline and how to credit researchers; (6) Bug bounty scope — which AI features are in scope, reward range by severity, out-of-scope behaviors. Provide the public disclosure policy template and the internal triage checklist.","tags":["security","responsible-disclosure","ai-safety"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-94","title":"Few-Shot Prompt Example Selection","prompt":"[Describe your AI use case and constraints]\n\nYou are optimizing the few-shot examples for the use case described above. Static, manually chosen few-shot examples are often suboptimal because they may not cover the input distribution and can bias the model. Design a dynamic few-shot example selection system: (1) Example library construction — how to build and maintain a library of high-quality (input, output) pairs covering diverse scenarios; quality criteria for inclusion; recommended library size; (2) Retrieval-based selection — at query time, retrieve the K most similar examples from the library using embedding similarity to the current input; implementation using FAISS or a vector database; (3) Diversity enforcement — beyond similarity, ensure selected examples are diverse (MMR algorithm to balance relevance and diversity); (4) Example ordering — empirical guidance on whether to place most-similar examples first or last relative to the current query; (5) Negative examples — when and how to include examples showing what NOT to do; (6) Example quality validation — automated quality check to detect library examples that cause the model to perform worse; (7) Library maintenance — how to add new examples, deprecate poor examples, and detect when the library needs expansion. Provide the dynamic selection implementation and the example quality scoring rubric.","tags":["few-shot","prompt-engineering","retrieval"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-95","title":"AI Technical Interview Preparation Guide","prompt":"Create a comprehensive preparation guide for AI engineering technical interviews at top technology companies. The guide targets candidates applying for ML Engineer, AI Engineer, and Applied Scientist roles who have 2-4 years of experience. Cover: (1) Machine learning fundamentals — the 10 most frequently tested concepts (bias-variance trade-off, regularization, gradient descent variants, backpropagation, cross-validation) with interview-ready explanations; (2) Deep learning and LLMs — transformer architecture questions, attention mechanism derivation, fine-tuning approaches, inference optimization, and RAG system design; (3) System design for ML — framework for answering ML system design questions (define problem → data → model → training → serving → evaluation → monitoring), with a worked example for a recommendation system; (4) Coding questions — common patterns (dynamic programming for sequence problems, graph algorithms for knowledge graphs, hash maps for feature caching) with Python solutions; (5) LLM-specific questions — prompt engineering trade-offs, evaluation methodology, hallucination mitigation, and agent architectures; (6) Behavioral questions — STAR format answers for AI ethics scenarios, handling model failures, and cross-functional collaboration; (7) Take-home project tips — structuring ML notebooks for maximum clarity and demonstrating production-readiness thinking. Include a 4-week study plan.","tags":["career","interview-prep","machine-learning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-96","title":"Streaming LLM Response Architecture","prompt":"Design a production streaming architecture for LLM responses in a web application that requires real-time token streaming to provide users with an immediate sense of progress on long-generation tasks. The application serves 10,000 concurrent users, runs on a serverless backend, and must handle streaming reliably across web, iOS, and Android clients. Architecture: (1) Server-Sent Events vs. WebSockets — compare for this use case (connection overhead, browser support, proxy compatibility, resumability); recommend with justification; (2) Backend streaming implementation — async Python streaming handler using the OpenAI/Anthropic streaming API, token buffer management, and error handling for mid-stream failures; (3) Serverless constraints — how to handle streaming in AWS Lambda or Vercel Edge Functions (response streaming support, timeout limits, cold start impact on TTFT); (4) Client implementation — JavaScript EventSource API usage, incremental markdown rendering, scroll behavior during streaming; (5) Reliability — what to do when a stream is interrupted mid-response (resumption strategy vs. restart); (6) Rate limiting — per-user streaming concurrency limits and queue management; (7) Mobile clients — efficient streaming implementation for iOS (URLSession) and Android (OkHttp) with battery and data usage considerations. Provide the backend streaming handler code and the client-side incremental rendering pattern.","tags":["streaming","architecture","web-development"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-97","title":"AI Ethics Impact Assessment","prompt":"[Describe your AI use case and constraints]\n\nConduct a systematic AI ethics impact assessment for the use case described above. This assessment should identify potential harms, affected stakeholders, and required safeguards before deployment. Assessment framework: (1) Stakeholder mapping — identify all groups affected by this AI system: direct users, indirect affected parties, those excluded from benefit, and society broadly; (2) Potential harms inventory — for each stakeholder group, enumerate potential harms across dimensions: physical, psychological, financial, privacy, autonomy, dignity, and fairness; assess likelihood and severity for each; (3) Bias and fairness analysis — identify protected characteristics relevant to this use case, specify fairness metrics to measure, and define acceptable disparity thresholds; (4) Transparency requirements — what information must be disclosed to users about AI involvement, data usage, and decision logic, and through what channels; (5) Power dynamics — does this system shift power in ways that disadvantage vulnerable groups? What mitigations are required? (6) Reversibility — are decisions made by this system contestable and reversible? If not, what additional oversight is required? (7) Mitigation plan — prioritized list of changes to system design, deployment policy, and monitoring needed to address identified harms. Provide the completed assessment table and deployment recommendation.","tags":["ai-ethics","impact-assessment","responsible-ai"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-98","title":"LLM Benchmark Design and Execution","prompt":"Design and execute a custom LLM benchmark for selecting the best model for a specialized domain: financial analysis and report generation for an asset management firm. Publicly available benchmarks (MMLU, HellaSwag) do not measure performance on the specific tasks this firm needs. Benchmark design: (1) Task specification — define 6 benchmark tasks: earnings summary generation, risk factor extraction, financial ratio calculation and interpretation, regulatory filing analysis, market commentary drafting, and comparative company analysis; (2) Dataset construction — for each task, create 50 test examples with human-annotated ground truth; data sources, annotation guidelines, and inter-annotator agreement requirements; (3) Metric design — task-specific metrics: extraction F1, calculation accuracy, fluency + factual accuracy for generated text (human evaluation rubric), citation precision; (4) Model selection — which models to benchmark (GPT-4o, Claude 3.5 Sonnet, Gemini 1.5 Pro, Llama-3-70B with financial fine-tune) and prompt normalization strategy; (5) Statistical rigor — confidence intervals, significance testing, minimum performance gap to declare a winner; (6) Cost-performance Pareto frontier — plot models on quality vs. cost per task and identify the optimal operating point. Provide the benchmark dataset schema and evaluation code structure.","tags":["benchmarking","evals","finance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-99","title":"Agentic Workflow Debugging and Observability","prompt":"Design a debugging and observability framework specifically for agentic AI workflows where failures are hard to diagnose because they emerge from complex interactions between the LLM, tools, state, and multi-step reasoning. Traditional debugging (logs + breakpoints) is insufficient for non-deterministic reasoning chains. Framework: (1) Execution trace format — structured representation of the complete agent execution: each thought, action, tool call, tool response, and state transition, with timestamps and token counts; (2) Trace storage and querying — how to store millions of execution traces and query for: all traces containing a specific tool failure, all traces where the agent looped more than 5 times, traces with unexpectedly long reasoning chains; (3) Visual trace explorer — design spec for a UI that shows the agent's reasoning as an interactive graph with node expansion; (4) Automated anomaly detection — patterns that indicate agent pathology: reasoning loop detection, goal drift detection (agent pursuing different objective than original), excessive tool calls for simple tasks; (5) Root cause analysis workflow — systematic process for diagnosing a production agent failure from trace data; (6) Prompt version correlation — linking agent behavior changes to specific prompt version deployments. Provide the trace schema and the loop detection algorithm.","tags":["agents","debugging","observability"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"aie-100","title":"The Future of AI Engineering: 2025-2030 Roadmap","prompt":"You are a principal AI engineer with deep production experience. Construct a detailed technical roadmap for how AI engineering practices will evolve from 2025 to 2030, grounded in current research trajectories and production realities. Cover: (1) Model capabilities trajectory — how frontier model capabilities (reasoning, long context, multimodality, agency) will change what is technically feasible and what engineering patterns will become obsolete; (2) Infrastructure evolution — how inference hardware (GPU/TPU/specialized AI accelerators), serving frameworks, and edge deployment will change the deployment landscape; (3) Prompt engineering future — as models become more capable, how will the craft of prompt engineering change? What will be automated vs. what will require deeper expertise? (4) Agent maturity — the engineering advances needed for agents to be reliably deployed in high-stakes autonomous workflows (better tool use, reliable long-horizon planning, verifiable reasoning); (5) Evaluation sophistication — how evaluation methodology will need to advance to keep pace with increasingly capable and general models; (6) New roles and skills — which AI engineering skills will appreciate vs. depreciate in value, and what new specializations will emerge; (7) Risks and failure modes — the systemic risks that will emerge as AI systems become more deeply integrated into critical infrastructure, and engineering mitigations. Conclude with the 5 highest-leverage skills for an AI engineer to develop today.","tags":["future","strategy","ai-engineering"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:15:00Z","featured":true},
];

